# Overview # {#intro}

Move semantics were introduced with C++11, and have improved the language in
many aspects. However they have also brought their lot of defects:

- `std::move` does not move ;
- moved-from objects are in an unspecified state, and more often than not,
    must not be reused ;
- constant objects cannot be moved ;
- classes that want to support move-semantics must either have a natural empty
    state (like strings, containers or smart pointers)
    or force one into their design, usually creating a loophole in their class invariant.

C++26 further introduced trivial relocatability, as an incomplete solution
to all those issues (only trivial relocation, and only for objects that
live in dynamic storage).

We propose to resolve all these issues by bringing support for relocation,
not only trivial relocation, to all objects. In particular to objects with an
automatic lifetime.

We define relocation as the operation of constructing a
new target object while simultaneously destructing the source object.
Relocation will happen thanks to a new keyword `reloc` and a new relocation
constructor (conventionally written in this paper as `T(T reloc)`).

Throughout this paper, we may use `T(T reloc)` as an exposition-only shorthand.
Formally, the first parameter is a decomposed parameter of type `T` (potentially
cv-qualified), and additional parameters are permitted if they have default
initializers; see [the declaration section](#reloc-ctor-declaration). The general
form is thus `T(T cv src reloc, ...)`, where `...` denotes only parameters with
default initializers.

```c++
// with: void foo(non_null<unique_ptr<T>>)

non_null<unique_ptr<T>> const ptr{new T};
ptr->do_stuff();
foo(reloc ptr);
// ptr cannot be reused or else the program is ill-formed
```

This tackles every flaw not solved by move-semantics:

- `reloc` does perform the relocation ;
- relocated objects cannot be re-used, or else the program is ill-formed ;
- relocation disregards cv-qualifiers, so constant objects can be relocated ;
- classes that want to support relocation can do so without breaking their
    class invariant, as the relocated-from object is simultaneously destructed.

Another major addition of this proposal is the object decomposition feature.
Object decomposition enables relocation of subobjects in compound types
(such as `std::pair`), including non-trivial relocation through user-defined
relocation constructors when needed, while keeping defaulted relocation
constructors sufficient for most classes.

```c++
pair<std::string const, int> const keyValue reloc = extractKV();
storeKey(reloc keyValue.first);
```
```c++
template <class First, class Second>
class MyPair
{
public:
    First first;
    Second second;

    // user-defined relocation constructor (could have been defaulted)
    MyPair(MyPair rhs reloc) : first{reloc rhs.first}, second{reloc rhs.second} {}
};
```

The `reloc` keyword in the object definition indicates that the object
is *decomposed*, which allows the relocation of its subobjects with `reloc`.

This proposal introduces:

- two new special member functions: a relocation constructor `T(T reloc)` and a relocation
    assignment operator `T& operator=(T)` or `T& operator=(T reloc)` ;
- a new keyword: `reloc` which backs a new operator and the object
    decomposition feature ;
- slight changes to overload resolution rules ;
- might introduce ABI breaks to some functions (opt-in).

This proposal does not introduce a new value category: instead it completes C++
tripartite value system as we propose relocation to happen from prvalues.

## Changes from previous revisions of P2785 ## {#changes}

### Changes from P2785R3 ### {#changes-from-r3}

#### Motivation #### {#changes-from-r3-motivation}

The motivation section is more complete, giving more explanations, describing
where today's C++ falls short, how we intend to improve, and each time illustrated with an
example.

#### Generalization of object decomposition #### {#changes-from-r3-dot-reloc}

One of the major changes we introduced is the generalization of the object decomposition
feature, which was introduced by the "decomposing functions".
The `reloc` keyword can now be used to decompose any object, not only
the `this` parameter of a member function.
This has several advantages:

- it greatly simplifies the writing and the understanding of the relocation constructor,
    relocation assignment operator and structured decomposition ;
- it simplifies the handling of relocate-only subobjects in user code ;
- it removes the *decomposing functions* feature as they are being replaced by
    a more generic object decomposition feature ;
- the whole proposal is more unified as all those changes are now built on top
    of the *object decomposition* feature.

#### Value parameter ownership rule #### {#changes-from-r3-value-param-ownership}

In P2785R3, we mandated that a function taking a relocate-only type as value
parameter **must** have control over its lifetime. This might break some ABIs
on rare occasions, but in a controlled way.

In R4, we take a slightly less conservative approach: *any function
that takes a parameter by value, whose type provides a relocation constructor*
**but no move constructor**, *must have control over the lifetime of that parameter.*

Discussion about this change is detailed in the
[reloc with function parameters](#reloc-with-function-param)
section.

#### Library changes #### {#changes-from-r3-stl}

In P2785R3, we enumerated all the desired changes in the STL to properly
support relocation. Now the suggested library changes only contain the bare
minimum for library writers. A more comprehensive list of changes
will be proposed separately, depending on the reception of the current proposal.

### Changes from older revisions ### {#changes-from-older}

R0, R1 and R2 revisions are internal versions bumps that were
never officially presented.

# Motivation # {#motivation}

## Relocation of automatic variables allows for safer code ## {#reloc-allows-safer-code}

### Support for always-valid types ### {#always-valid-code}

Tony Hoare stated that the introduction of the null reference was his
"billion dollar mistake". Null pointers are a common source of software failures,
often causing software crashes and interruption of services.

We all know that when using a pointer, we need to ensure of its validity first.
Unfortunately, most of C++ classes suffer from the same design flaw.
Because of move-semantics, when an object has been moved, it is in a valid
but unknown state. If a moved-from object is to be reused, the object
must first be reassigned to some known state before doing anything else with it.
This approach is so error-prone, that many guidelines advise to simply drop the
moved-from object.

Because of this, in C++ it is not possible to write a class that provides a
very basic guarantee: a pointer that is always valid.
It is not entirely true as we do have `gsl::non_null<std::unique_ptr>`,
but as this class provides no move constructor and no copy constructor,
its usefulness is greatly diminished. It can only be used as an immovable
stack variable, or stored in a node-based container.

Move-semantics requires the moved-from object to be still valid. This greatly
complicates some class design. Hence our always-valid pointer class can hardly
have a move constructor: an efficient move constructor would set the address
of the moved-from object to nullptr, but the class invariant forbids this value.
(An inefficient move constructor would perform an extra memory allocation,
and would leave the moved-from object with a valid pointer, but pointing to
a moved-from object... But no one wants to pay the cost of an extra memory
allocation for an object that will be dropped right away.)

This can be extended to any class which aims to provide always-valid access to some
resource. None of those can be correctly implemented today in C++ with move semantics,
unless everyone complies with the status-quo rule that a moved-from object must
not be reused. Relocation fixes the problem completely. As relocation **moves**
and **destroys** in the same operation, the relocated-from object ceases to
exist and cannot be reused. No invalid state must be forced into its class design,
which would break its class invariants.

For instance, an always-valid pointer class can be:
```c++
template <class T>
class box
{
public:
    template <class... Args>
    explicit box(Args&&... args) : _ptr{new T{std::forward<Args>(args)...}} {}
    ~box() { delete _ptr; }

    box(box const&) = delete;
    box(box&&) = delete;
    box(box reloc) = default;

    box& operator=(box const&) = delete;
    box& operator=(box&&) = delete;
    box& operator=(box reloc) = default;

    T* operator->() const { return _ptr; }
    T& operator*() const { return *_ptr; }

    // exchange this with a new box, return its former value
    box exchange(box = {}) noexcept;

private:
    T* _ptr;
};
```

This class achieves its purpose in very few lines. It is not part of this
proposal (an STL version would likely feature support for custom allocators and
custom destructors). However we believe that relocation is a major prerequisite
for such classes. Such `box` classes and the likes landing someday in C++ is a
strong motivation for this proposal.

#### The design loophole of std::indirect and std::polymorphic #### {#std-indirect}

`std::indirect` and `std::polymorphic` both got adopted into C++26. However
both classes suffer from a serious design flow: they attempted to be always-valid
classes yet have a move constructor.

One cannot construct an empty `std::indirect` (or `std::polymorphic`) object,
but yet it can become valueless after a move operation, and the
`valueless_after_move()` member function can be used to verify its state.

Both classes would have greatly benefited from a deleted move constructor and
a defaulted relocation constructor. This way, both classes would indeed have
been always-valid, and no member function would have been needed to
check that aberrant state.

C++26 STL hardening may mitigate this, but it will always be an inferior solution
compared to what we propose: a solution at type system level which will be
statically checked.

### Simplify class writing ### {#simple-class-writing}

Currently in C++, if you write a struct which uniquely owns a resource, you have
to write a lot of boilerplates:

```c++
struct A {
    A(A &&src) : ptr(src.ptr) {
        src.ptr = nullptr;
    }
    A(const A &) = delete;
    A &operator=(A &&src) {
        if (&src != this)
        {
            delete ptr;
            ptr = src.ptr;
            src.ptr = nullptr;
        }
        return *this;
    }
    A &operator=(const A &) = delete;
    ~A() {
        delete ptr;
    }

private:
    T *ptr;
};
```

And this is just one data member, the required amount of duplicated efforts are
correlated by the count of uniquely-owned resources in the wrapper type.
Requiring the users to manually write all the assignments and then setting the
source object back to null state can be tiresome and bug-prone. All the efforts
went in, just to satisfy a niche scenario (use after move).

Also note the address check in the move assignment operator. In our experience,
this check is often forgotten in user code, leaving the door open for potential
vulnerabilities. This check must always be done, also to satisfy another niche
scenario (`a = std::move(b)`, in the unlikely event where one is a reference on
the other).

With relocation constructors, this is greatly simplified, because now there's
less situations (moved-from state) to worry about:

```c++
struct A {
    A(A reloc) = default;
    A(const A &) = delete;
    A(A &&) = delete;
    A& operator=(A rhs reloc) {
        delete ptr;
        ptr = rhs.ptr;
        return *this;
    }
    A &operator=(const A &) = delete;
    A &operator=(A &&) = delete;
    ~A() {
        delete ptr;
    }

private:
    T *ptr;
};
```

In addition, the prvalue semantics further simplifies class writing:
the address check in the assignment
operator `if (&src != this)` is no longer needed. Indeed, thanks to prvalues,
it is impossible for the `src` parameter to occupy the same address as `this`.

### reloc is a safer alternative to std::move ### {#reloc-vs-move-motivation}

The `reloc` operator is a safer alternative to `std::move`. The `reloc` operator
prevents use-after-relocation errors, and allows to [relocate constant objects](#reloc-likes-const).

`reloc` can be used as a drop-in replacement to `std::move`, with [similar performance](#parameter-passing):

```c++
// with std::string a, b
foo(std::move(a));
vector.push_back(std::move(b));

// becomes:
foo(reloc a);
vector.push_back(reloc b);
```

There are still a couple of cases where `std::move` is still valuable:

- when applied to a data member, or a non complete object. In which case, the
    equivalent `reloc` expression would be ill-formed (see
    [relocating data members](#reloc-data-members) for alternatives).
- when use after move is explicitly intended. If such expressions are promoted
    to `reloc`, then the expression that reuses the relocated objects will be
    ill-formed. Note that such `std::move` calls today would benefit of being
    replaced by `std::exchange` calls, hence no *moved-from* objects are handled
    in user code.

Note though, that a mere find and replace from `std::move` to `reloc` in a code
base will create compilation errors on the above cases (no quiet code break).


### Relocation loves constant objects ### {#reloc-likes-const}

Automatic objects are recommended by various guidelines to be constant, since a constant object
will not change throughout its lifetime, simplifying reasoning about program state both for
humans (coders, reviewers, maintainers) and for machines (compilers, tooling).
Allowing an object to be `const` throughout its lifetime but give up its
resources at the end of its lifetime allows for better, safer code.

#### Constant automatic variables can be relocated #### {#reloc-automatic-var}

`const` usefulness is greatly diminished because of move-semantics,
since the move constructor cannot work with a constant source object.
Hence for those, the program silently falls back to calling their copy
constructor...

```c++
// with: void bar(A);

A const a = someFunc();
bar(std::move(a)); // silently calls A's copy ctor, performing a deep copy...
```

As a direct consequence:

- some developers will use `const` to naively declare constant objects as so - *as one would
    expect from the language* - while being unaware of the side-effects. This may
    lead to unoptimized code, with the copy constructor being called *silently*
    while `std::move` is *explicitly called* ;
- knowledgeable developers will rather not use it if they can foresee the
    negative side-effect, which will improve performance at the cost of code
    readability.

This is a missed opportunity and it leads to poorer code. With relocation
semantics, we aim to make `const` shine again.
Constant objects can be relocated, they are just destroyed when done so.

```c++
A const a = someFunc();
bar(reloc a);
```

NOTE: The above snippet suffers from no pessimization even if `A` only has a
move constructor and no relocation constructor. Indeed `reloc a` promotes `a`
to a prvalue, which occupies the same storage as `a`, and stripped of
the cv-qualifiers that previously applied to it.
It will effectively call `A`'s move constructor, with a deconstified rvalue
reference on `a`. This further proves that `reloc` is a semantically safer and
more expressive alternative to `std::move`.

We also have to consider that the Immediately Invoked Function Expressions (IIFE)
decouples the possibilities to declare constant variables (even when they have
a complex initialization routine), were they not hindered down
by `std::move`.

Consider the following code snippets, and see how `const`+`reloc` improves the
code:

<table>
<tr><th>Before</th><th>Constified + relocation (IIFE)</th></tr>
<tr>
<td valign="top">
```c++
void foo(A a, B b, C c);

void bar()
{
  A a = getA();

  B b = getB(a);
  b.processData();

  C c = getC(a.token(), b);
  c.fetchAll();
  c.processData();
  c.finalize();

  foo(std::move(a), std::move(b),
    std::move(c));
}
```
</td>
<td valign="top">
```c++
void foo(A a, B b, C c);

void bar()
{
  A const a = getA();

  B const b = std::invoke([&] {
    B b = getB(a);
    b.processData();
    return b;
  });

  C const c = std::invoke([&] {
    C c = getC(a.token(), b);
    c.fetchAll();
    c.processData();
    c.finalize();
    return c;
  });

  foo(reloc a, reloc b, reloc c);
}
```
</td>
</tr></table>

NOTE: We expect the `reloc` operator to also multiply the
possibilities to declare constant variables, with or without the IIFE idiom:

<table>
<tr><th>Constified + relocation (no IIFE)</th></tr>
<tr>
<td valign="top">
```c++
void foo(A a, B b, C c);

void bar()
{
  A const a = getA();

  B b_mut = getB(a);
  b_mut.processData();
  B const b = reloc b_mut;
  // b_mut can no longer be used

  C c_mut = getC(a.token(), b);
  c_mut.fetchAll();
  c_mut.processData();
  c_mut.finalize();
  C const c = reloc c_mut;
  // c_mut can no longer be used

  foo(reloc a, reloc b, reloc c);
}
```
</td>
</tr></table>

#### Constant data members better expresses class invariant #### {#constant-data-member}

In addition constant member functions and constant data members in class design
enable to explicitly state class invariants. This subsection adds on the
[Support for the always-valid types](#always-valid-code) and the
[Simplify class writing](#simple-class-writing) sections: a constant data member,
due to its constant nature, cannot be set to an empty state. Hence it is intrinsically
backing an always-valid state, and simplified constructor/destructor writing.

It's not uncommon to find in production code like this:

```c++
class SomeType
{
public:
    // some parameterized constructor
    // SomeType(...)

    SomeType(SomeType&&) = default;

private:
    std::string const _key1;
    std::string const _key2;
    int _val;
};
```

Here the developer innocently qualified `_key1` and `_key2` as constant.
In their mind, their value was never to change in the lifetime of the object.
This being part of the class invariant, it only feels natural to enforce the
class invariant in the code, with the `const` qualifier.
However as we have seen, this will cause deep copies of the constant objects
in the move constructor.

This issue is again solved with relocation (no gotchas!):

<table>
<tr><th>Before (fixed)</th><th>After</th></tr>
<tr>
<td valign="top">
```c++
class SomeType
{
public:
  // some parameterized constructor
  // SomeType(...)

  SomeType(SomeType&&) = default;

private:
  std::string _key1; /* const but cannot
    mark it so because of the
    move constructor */
  std::string _key2; /* same as _key1 */

  int _val;
};
```
</td>
<td valign="top">
```c++
class SomeType
{
public:
  // some parameterized constructor
  // SomeType(...)

  SomeType(SomeType reloc) = default;

private:
  std::string const _key1;
  std::string const _key2;
  int _val;
};
```
</td>
</tr></table>

See how the code becomes clearer: class invariant can be written in the code
instead of comments, which leads to a code that is both more readable and
robust.

## memberwise_trivially_relocatable is not a solution ## {#trivial-relocation-is-no-solution}

C++26 introduces `memberwise_trivially_relocatable`. This form of relocation which only
affects objects that live in dynamic storage, and only support trivial relocation, proves to be an incomplete solution.
It is precisely providing support for both automatic storage objects and
non-trivial relocation which
enables us to bring the benefits we described. If either of those is missing, we
cannot have:

- [always valid-types](#always-valid-code) ;
- [simplified class writing](#simple-class-writing) ;
- and [decoupled opportunities for constant objects](#reloc-likes-const).

Indeed, those benefits can only emerge if we can write a non-immovable class
without a move constructor. If we only supported dynamic storage objects, then
such types could never be handled in automatic variables, creating a serious
discrepancy (farewell `std::box`).

Support for trivial relocation only is also not an option. The primary concern
is that of composability; an aggregate of a self-referential type that cannot be
trivially relocated (e.g. `std::string` or `std::list` in some
implementations) and a relocate-only type (e.g. a future `std::box`) can neither
be relocated by move-and-destroy nor trivially relocated.
(e.g. `std::pair<std::string, std::box<int>>` could never be relocated in
some implementation, not even moved). Thus non-trivial relocation emerges as a requirement.

## Pave the way to linear type support (higher RAII) ## {#linear-types}

C++ does not support linear types, and this proposal does not aim to provide support
for it. However it has been pointed out by Sidney Cogdill that this proposal
is a big step to bring support for linear types in the language.
This is a happy accident, as it was not initially intended.

A few other languages, such as [Vale](https://vale.dev/), support linear types.
The Vale language highlights this feature as [higher RAII](https://verdagon.dev/blog/higher-raii-7drl).
[Another blog post](https://verdagon.dev/blog/higher-raii-uses-linear-types) lists all the benefits of such types.

To briefly summarize them:

- a linear type may have several destructors ;
- a linear object is an object that must be destroyed explicitly, or else the
    program is ill-formed.

The benefits of this approach is that, if an object requires some action to be
taken before its destruction, then that action can be realized in one of its
destructors and can never be forgotten by the developer, as a destructor must be
explicitly invoked.

For instance, imagine you have a `DBTransaction` class,
with two destructors: `commit` or `rollback`. You are then ensured that every
transaction in the code will either be committed or rolled back, and none
will be forgotten. To get a more complete view of linear types, the reader is
encouraged to read the mentioned blog posts.

The current proposal brings C++ closer to linear type support because [object decomposition](#decomposed-object)
offers an alternative way to end the lifetime of an object, without going
through a destructor.
The `DBTransaction` class
we mentioned earlier could then be implemented:

```c++
struct DBTransaction
{
// [...] data members and other functions

    void rollback(this DBTransaction self reloc)
    //                                    ^ marks the decomposition of self
    {
        self._cxn.rollback();
        // as self is decomposed, its destructor is not invoked
        // (but all its subobjects are still destroyed)
    }
    void commit(this DBTransaction self reloc)
    //                                  ^ marks the decomposition of self
    {
        self._cxn.commit();
        // as self is decomposed, its destructor is not invoked
        // (but all its subobjects are still destroyed)
    }
    ~DBTransaction()
    {
        _cxn.rollback();
    }
};

void foo(DBTransaction transaction)
{
    (reloc transaction).commit();
}
```

A future proposal could build upon this one and introduce full linear type
support. It could do so by:

- adding an extra contextual keyword in the class definition to define a class
    as linear (e.g. `class DBTransaction linear`), and ;
- by stating that the destructor of this
    class is never implicitly called (except during stack-unwinding) and the program
    is ill-formed if the instance is not destroyed explicitly or relocated.

## Teachability ## {#motivation-teachability}

It is a matter of some confusion for learners that a "moved-from" object still exists and is
accessible, but has an unspecified state. This leads to so-called "use-after-move" bugs, and
requires static analysis passes and coding guidelines to prevent. "What does `std::move` do?"
is, regrettably, a common "gotcha" question in a certain style of coding interview. In this
matter C++ compares unfavorably to other languages where the "move" operation is destructive
(if trivial) and bars further access to the source object.

# Nomenclature # {#nomenclature}

We refer to the <a href="https://wg21.link/intro.defs">Terms and definitions</a> of the C++ Standard,
and to nomenclature introduced therein,
in particular the <a href="https://wg21.link/intro.object">object model</a>.
In addition, we define:

## Source and target objects ## {#src-and-target}

Relocation is the act of constructing a new instance while ending the lifetime of an existing one.
This allows destructively stealing its resources, if any.

The new instance is called the *target object*.
The existing instance whose lifetime is ended and whose resources
are stolen is called the *source object*.

## Early destructible ## {#early-destructible}

The lifetime of function parameters is implementation-defined in C++, but it
is of most importance with relocation. Depending on the function call convention
or ABI, the function may or may not be in charge of the lifetime of its parameters.

An object is said to be *early destructible* with regards to a function `f`
if:

- the object is a local variable of `f`, not ref-qualified, and with automatic
    storage ;
- or the object is a parameter of `f`, passed by value, and that `f`
    has control over its lifetime ;
- or the object's destructor is a no-op.

We denote two main parameter passing conventions:

- *caller-destroy*: the call site is in charge of the lifetime of the
    value parameter passed in the function call ;
- *callee-destroy*: the function itself is in charge of the lifetime of its
    value parameters ;

Depending on the ABI choice, the convention can be per parameter, or apply
to all the function parameters. Other conventions may exist, and they are
still compatible with this proposal.

For instance, in a function with caller-destroy convention, none of its value
parameters are *early destructible*.
Likewise, with callee-destroy, all of its value parameters are
*early destructible*.
And last, regardless of the convention, all not ref-qualified, local variables
with automatic storage are *early destructible*.

# Comparison with existing proposals # {#compare}

This proposal introduces the `reloc` keyword, which allows users to explicitly
and safely relocate local variables in their code base.

This proposal is also one of the few (with [[P0308R0]]), to tackle the case of
relocate-only types. The `reloc` keyword thus becomes necessary to safely pass
around such objects in user code.

Also, all these proposals (but [[P0308R0]]) aim to optimize the move and destruct
operations into a single memcpy. But there are places where this optimization
could not happen, and we are left with suboptimized move constructor and destructor calls.

The relocation constructor that we propose offers a customization point, and
especially allows for a more optimized relocation technique than move and destruct.
If trivial, then it can be optimized into a `memcpy`.

## P0023R0: Relocator: Efficiently moving objects by Denis Bider ## {#compare-P0023R0}

The Relocator introduced in [[P0023R0]] is similar to the proposed relocation constructor.
However P0023R0's Relocator is not viewed as a constructor. Instead, it is a special
function that can be invoked in lieu of move plus destroy where possible.

However this brings again suboptimal support for relocate-only types. Indeed [[P0023R0]]
does not force relocation to happen in all cases, and falls back to move+destroy paradigm
when relocation cannot happen.

A typical example is when relocating a function parameter:

```c++
void sink(T);
void foo(T x) { sink(x); }
```

Here, under the terms of [[P0023R0]], relocation may not happen because of ABI
constraints (if `x` is not *early destructible*).
This will cause `foo` to fallback to a move+deferred destroy paradigm.

This proposal has another take on this issue: if `T` has a relocation constructor
but no move constructor, then any function
that takes a `T` parameter by value is required to have an ABI that allows it to
relocate
its input parameter (e.g. callee-destroy ABI).

This proposal also introduces the `reloc` keyword that is here to trigger
the relocation, and protect against the reuse of the relocated object.
The solution then becomes:

```c++
void sink(T);
void foo(T x) { sink(reloc x); /* x can no longer be used. */ }
```

Moreover, the proposed syntax for invoking [[P0023R0]]'s relocator is low-level and
prone to error:

```c++
T x;
alignas(T) char buf[sizeof(T)];
T* y = new (buf) >>T(x);
```

Here the lifetime of `x` has been ended as if by a destructor call, but the language
is not aware of this,
so firstly the user may erreoneously refer to `x` as if it was within its lifetime,
so firstly the user may erroneously refer to `x` as if it was within its lifetime,
and secondly if an object is not recreated in `x` by the time the block
exits, the behavior is undefined by <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/basic.life#9">[basic.life]/9</a>.
Likewise, the language is not aware of the existence of `*y` so will not call its
destructor; the behavior is then possibly undefined under <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/basic.life#5">[basic.life]/5</a>.
In contrast, the present proposal would write the above as:

```c++
T x;
T y = reloc x;
```

Here the use of the `reloc` keyword ensures that the language is aware that the lifetime
of `x` has finished, so the destructor only of `y` is called at scope exit.

## N4158: Destructive Move by Pablo Halpern ## {#compare-N4158}

[[N4158]] proposes a customizable function `std::uninitialized_destructive_move`,
that is somewhat similar to the relocation constructor, but is a pure library solution.

It has several drawbacks :

- relocation can only happen if this function is called explicitly. Typically this function
    would be called in container implementation. But then we cannot relocate
    local variables with this.
- users can write their own `uninitialized_destructive_move` overload, but this
    is terrible for composability. Classes that have a subobject with a
    custom `uninitialized_destructive_move` overload do not get a
    `uninitialized_destructive_move` overload generated for free.
- `uninitialized_destructive_move` cannot be default-generated with memberwise
    relocation.

## P1029R3: move = bitcopies by Niall Douglas ## {#compare-P1029R3}

[[P1029R3]] enables a special bitcopies move constructor for types that are
trivially relocatable.

We have already pointed out that [trivial relocation is not enough](#trivial-relocation-is-no-solution).
Because of that point alone, it does not solve any of the issues we mentioned.

## P0308R0: Valueless Variants Considered Harmful by Peter Dimov ## {#compare-P0308R0}

We only consider the "pilfering" proposition from [[P0308R0]].
P0308R0's pilfering shares some similarities with the current proposal,
as it is an attempt to support relocate-only types as a pure library solution.

We believe a language solution is best suited here:

- the source object is effectively destroyed by the relocation: its destructor
    is not called. This can hardly be achieved by a library solution ;
- the `reloc` keyword makes sure the relocated object is not reused,
    while `std::pilfer` does not ;
- the pilfering constructor is inconvenient to write as we need to unwrap from
    `std::pilfered` and rewrap to propagate to base classes and data members ;
- as a library solution, the pilfering constructor cannot be defaulted ;
- trivial relocation is not possible with pilfering, which makes [[P0308R0]] miss
    the performance boost that is so longed for.

## D2839R1: Nontrivial Relocation via a New *owning reference* Type by Brian Bi and Joshua Berne ## {#compare-D2839}

[[D2839R1]] is very close in spirit and mechanism to this paper. We consider the
addition of an "owning reference" to be an unnecessary complication to the type
system that would impose a burden throughout library code. We refute the claim
that a prvalue occupying storage constitutes a fourth value category; in our
model, the lvalue that previously occupied that storage ceases to exist and a
new prvalue is formed, which may come to occupy that storage (if relocation is
elided) or distinct storage (in the general case).

We are intrigued by the claim that an owning reference can provide superior
performance when passing a variable up the stack, compared to repeatedly relocating
it from caller to callee, but believe that in practice such gains would be minimal
and liable to be eliminated via optimization, while our model offers the possibility
of improved locality, since the relocated object will reside directly in the leafmost
function's stack as opposed to being accessible only via indirection.
Alternatively, this concern can be regarded as an implementation choice.
Implementations where only the address of the object is
passed down from caller to callee are [still possible with our model](#0-copy).

Otherwise, the paper has some minor differences in naming (e.g. `std::force_relocate`
for what we call `std::reloc_and_uninitialize`) that could easily be reconciled in either
direction.

# Proposed language changes # {#lg-changes}

## reloc operator ## {#reloc-operator}

This paper suggests to introduce a new keyword, named `reloc`. `reloc` acts as a
unary operator that can be applied to named, local, complete objects (in other
words: function-local non-static variables and, with some restrictions,
function parameters and
identifiers introduced through the syntax of structured binding declarations).

`reloc obj` does the following:

- if `obj` is ref-qualified, then performs perfect-forwarding
    (as if by `static_cast<decltype(obj)>(obj)`) ;
- otherwise returns a temporary obtained from the source object, constructed
    from the relocation, move or copy constructor.
    - If the relocation constructor
        was called, then the source object has been destroyed.
    - If not, then the destructor of the source object will be implicitly
        invoked as soon as the implementation permits, if possible at the end
        of the full-expression evaluation ;
- in all cases, marks the "early" end-of-scope of the variable `obj`, preventing
    reuses of the variable.

### reloc to perfectly forward all value categories ### {#reloc-perfect-fwd}

`reloc` can be used on ref-qualified objects to enable perfect forwarding.
If the source object is ref-qualified, then it performs the following cast:
`static_cast<decltype(obj)>(obj)`.

This makes `reloc` the one operator to use to perfectly forward objects, whether
they are ref-qualified or not.
It also prevents from use-after-move and use-after-relocation errors.

### reloc in constexpr ### {#reloc-constexpr}

`reloc` can be used in constexpr functions and constant-evaluated expressions.

### reloc produces a prvalue ### {#reloc-value-change}

Unlike `std::move`, `reloc` does perform some form of relocation.
Through misuse of language, we say that `reloc` returns a temporary object,
created by relocation, move or copy constructor. In truth,
given that the source object is not ref-qualified, `reloc` merely
changes the value category of the source object from lvalue to prvalue
(if the source object is ref-qualified, see [above](#reloc-perfect-fwd)).
The prvalue produced by `reloc` occupies the same storage as that of the
source object.

It is this value category change that *may* trigger the creation of a temporary
object. However, optimizations may happen that will elide the creation of the
temporary entirely.

If the temporary is materialized, then it is ideally constructed by the relocation
constructor, destroying the source object in the process. However, `reloc` does
not force the use of the relocation constructor. The constructor which will be used
to create the temporary is selected by overload resolution. `reloc` merely enables
the relocation constructor to be selected because of the value category change
(the relocation constructor, conventionally written `T(T reloc)` in this paper,
will be preferred
when constructed from a prvalue, with our new [overload resolution rules](#overload-resolution)).

### Relocation constructor discardment with function parameters ### {#reloc-with-function-param}

Function parameters' lifetime ownership is implementation-defined in C++.
Depending on the ABI in use, a function parameter may be destroyed at caller-site
(upon function return, at the end of the full-expression evaluation),
or at callee-site (before the function exits).

Enforcing the relocation constructor when `reloc` is used on a function
parameter, which would effectively destroy the function parameter,
will not be compatible with all existing ABIs (see Itanium ABI for
instance). We could propose this nonetheless, by stating that the relocation
constructor must be used if declared.
However we fear this would propagate ABI changes with too little control
and the proposal would meet too much resistance to go further.

We could take a very conservative approach, and mandate the ABI change
for *relocate-only* value parameters. Such types don't exist today, so the
likelihood of breaking any existing ABI is relatively small. However this may
lead to suboptimized code, where `reloc` would perform worse than move semantics.
Consider now the following code:


<table>
<tr><th>With move semantics</th><th>With relocation</th></tr>
<tr>
<td valign="top">
```c++
struct A
{
    // [...]
    A(A const&) = default;
    A(A&&) = default;
};

void bar(A);
void foo(A a)
{
    bar(std::move(a));
}
```
</td>
<td valign="top">
```c++
struct A
{
    // [...]
    A(A const&) = default;
    A(A&&) = delete;
    A(A reloc) = default;
};

void bar(A);
void foo(A a)
{
    bar(reloc a);
}
```
</td>
</tr>
</table>

Since `A` has a copy constructor, it is not relocate-only. With the conservative
approach, we have no guarantee
that `foo` has the ownership of `a`. Hence `reloc a` here may simply call the copy
constructor, as the ABI may not permit otherwise.

To prevent such a pessimization, developers will be tempted to add a move constructor
to `A`, ensuring that at least the move constructor is called in such cases.
Doing so goes against what we are trying to achieve with this proposal. One
of our main motivation points is that the move constructor doesn't fit all classes
that could yet still be moved. We don't want users to declare one anyway
to avoid pitfalls.

Thus in an attempt to make a compromise between ABI compatibility and acceptable
support for relocation, we take a slightly less conservative approach.
We would like function parameters passed by value, whose type declares
a relocation constructor but no move constructor, to be *early destructible*.
As the C++ standard leaves this area implementation-defined, we are going to force
this another way, although the effect is the same:

> **Rule:** In a temporary materialization, if the `reloc` operand is a function parameter passed by value, and its type
    provides an eligible move constructor, then the relocation constructor (if any)
    may be discarded during overload resolution. Whether the relocation constructor
    is discarded is *implementation-defined*.

Note: *eligible* is defined in
<a href="http://wg21.link/special#def:special_member_function,eligible">[special]</a>.

As consequences of that rule, we get the following:

- *early destructible* source objects are always guaranteed that
    their relocation constructor (if eligible) is called ;
- source objects which are not *early destructible*, and have both an eligible move
    and relocation constructor, will not be relocated. Instead the move constructor
    will be invoked by `reloc`.
- source objects which are function parameters, and have an eligible relocation
    constructor but no move constructor, are forcibly *early destructible*.

NOTE: Types which have a relocation constructor but no move constructor
still don't exist today, so the likelihood of breaking existing ABIs is still
small.

With this updated rule, `reloc a` from our previous example will effectively
call the relocation constructor. And users can safely migrate away from the move
constructor to the relocation constructor if they wish to without any gotchas.

Note that this rule still isn't optimal. Consider the following code,
having in mind a putative `std::pair` with a relocation constructor:

```c++
void bar(pair<const string, int>);

void foo(pair<const string, int> val)
{
    val.second *= 2;
    bar(reloc val);
}
```

`std::pair` has the three constructors, so our rule does not apply. The ownership
of `val` by `foo` is implementation-defined. If `foo` doesn't own `val`, then
`reloc val` will call the move constructor, making a full string copy
(because of `const`). Contrary to our previous example which motivated us to
update the parameter ownership rule,
here `reloc` may call `std::move` so we haven't worsened the situation.

### Early end of scope ### {#reloc-src-obj-lifetime}

As we have seen `reloc` may trigger a call to the relocation constructor.
At the moment of the call, the lifetime of the source object has ended.
Thus it is unsafe to reuse it after the `reloc` expression completes.

To prevent uses of a potentially destructed object, we state that the program is
**ill-formed** if the source object is reused after being passed to `reloc`,
regardless of whether the relocation constructor was called. For a detailed
explanation of how this is enforced, please refer to [the dedicated section](#ill-formed-reuse).

NOTE: The rest of this section only applies to `reloc` operands that are
not ref-qualified.

That early end of scope provides us with a strong guarantee. We know that the
source object cannot be reused, and as such we may be tempted to call the
destructor of the source object early (in cases where the relocation constructor
was not selected). For instance, we may want to destroy the
source object at the end of the full-expression evaluation, when temporaries are
destroyed. It would be the natural thing to do:

- for consistency, if the object had been relocated (with the relocation constructor),
    then it already reached its end of life. The same could be expected for
    copy and move constructor ;
- the source object cannot be reused anyway, why defer its destruction?

Unfortunately this cannot be done without breaking some ABIs, especially because
the source object may not be *early destructible*.
Let us see what happens if we allow the early destruction of function parameters
to be *implementation-defined*:

- If `reloc` calls the copy constructor:
    all the resources owned by the source object are *copied* to the target object.
    As such if the destructor has side-effects, they will be applied twice, once
    for the destruction of each object. Depending on the ABI, the destructor
    of the source object may be called at the end of full-expression, or deferred
    until control returns to the caller.
    We can see that the destruction time of the source object would be
    *implementation-defined* (as of today). Remember that we might have side-effects from the
    destructor, so the moment those side-effects will be applied will also
    be *implementation-defined*.
- If `reloc` calls the move constructor:
    all the resources owned by the source object are *transferred* to the target
    object. As such if the destructor has side-effects, we expect them to be
    applied only once, at the destruction of the target object. We expect the
    source object destructor to detect the *moved-from state* and return, without
    triggering any side-effects.
    Under those reasonably safe assumptions, it doesn't matter when the source
    object is destroyed, be it at its natural end of scope, or at the end of the
    `reloc` full-expression.

As of today, the lifetime of function parameters is already *implementation-defined*.
As such, code that relies on the timing of a destructor side-effects of a
function parameter is already *implementation-defined*.
We don't feel we will worsen the
situation with `reloc`, even with copy-only source objects.
We prefer to keep this behavior *implementation-defined*, same as
today, except that the source object may also be destructed early, at the end
of the full-expression evaluation that contained `reloc`.

In conclusion, we establish the following rule when a `reloc` expression
is evaluated:

> **Rule:** If a `reloc` operand (the source object) is not relocated by its
>   relocation constructor, and:
>   - the source object is not a function parameter: then
>       its lifetime ends (i.e. its destructor is implicitly invoked) at the
>       end of the full-expression evaluation containing the evaluated `reloc` expression ;
>   - the source object is a function parameter: then
>       its destructor must be implicitly invoked at the end of the full-expression
>       containing the evaluated `reloc` expression,
>       or at the caller site at the end of the
>       full-expression evaluation. Which it is is *implementation-defined*.

We acknowledge that with `reloc` expressions we allow for more code to be executed between
the end of the full-expression which contained `reloc`,
and when control returns to the caller site.
One might argue that we exacerbate this destruction time discrepancy between
implementations. However please keep in mind that:

- this only concerns copy-only value parameters, whose destructor have a side-effect, and
    are passed to `reloc` ;
- any arbitrary amount of code could still be executed today upon function exit,
    at the end of the full-expression evaluation that made the function call,
    at caller-site. Indeed, all function parameters need to be destructed,
    and their destruction order remains *implementation-defined* as of today,
    leaving the door open for any arbitrary code from a destructor,
    to be executed before the destructor of our source object ;
- code that relies on a precise destruction timing of a function parameter must
    be avoided as this timing is *implementation-defined*, even today.

### Illegal uses of reloc ### {#reloc-illegal-uses}

A `reloc` expression is ill-formed if any of the following conditions is met:

- its parameter is not the name of a variable, or a direct subobject of a
    decomposed object ;
- the source object is not a *complete object* ;
- the source object is not ref-qualified and does not have local storage
    (i.e. not a local function variable and not a function parameter passed by
    value) ;
- the source object is not ref-qualified and does not have an accessible relocation,
    move, or copy constructor. **Exception**: if the source object is not a function
    parameter, and the `reloc` expression is [discarded](#discarded-reloc-expr),
    then the source object is permitted to have none of the 3 constructors ;
- the source object is a structured binding (and not an identifier introduced in a
    <a href="#structured-decomposition">structured decomposition declaration</a>) ;
- the source object is a lambda capture (unless the lambda closure object has been [decomposed](#decompose-lambda)) ;
- the source object is a virtual base of a [decomposed object](#decomposed-state) ;


In particular, the `reloc` expression is well-formed if the source object is a
member of a function-local anonymous union.

For instance:
```cpp
void foo(std::string str);
std::string get_string();
std::pair<std::string, std::string> get_strings();

std::string gStr = "static string";

void bar(void)
{
    std::string str = "test string";
    foo(reloc str); // OK: relocation will happen given that std::string has a reloc ctor
    foo(reloc gStr); // ill-formed: gStr does not have local storage

    std::pair p{std::string{}, std::string{}};
    foo(reloc p.first); // ill-formed: p.first is not a complete object (p should have been decomposed)

    foo(reloc get_string()); // ill-formed: not the name of variable
    foo(reloc get_strings().first); // ill-formed: not a complete object, and not the name of variable
}

void foobar(const std::string& str)
{
    foo(reloc str); // OK: str is passed by reference.
    // Note that the lifetime of the referent of str is unaffected.
}
void foobar(std::string* str)
{
    foo(reloc *str); // ill-formed: *str is not the name of variable
}
void foobar2(std::string* str)
{
    foobar(reloc str); // OK, the pointer itself is relocated (not the pointed value)
}

class A
{
    std::string _str;
public:
    void bar()
    {
        foo(reloc _str); // ill-formed: _str is not a complete object and does not have local storage
    }
};
```

### Discarded reloc expressions ### {#discarded-reloc-expr}

This section discusses the effect of discarded `reloc` expressions in cases
where its operand is not ref-qualified. Such an expression is for instance
`reloc obj;`

We propose such expressions to have the same effect as ` { auto _ = reloc obj; } `.
A temporary is constructed from the source object and is then destructed at scope
exit (that is right-away). The source object is destructed right-away,
or at later stage if it's a function parameter (when exactly is *implementation-defined*).
We will discuss here the effects of such expressions.

Materializing a temporary whose only goal is to be
destroyed is suboptimal.
We might be tempted to optimize away the temporary
materialization, and only call the destructor of the source object.

This optimization is *mandatory* if the source object is immovable
(none of the three constructors are eligible) and not a function parameter.
In other cases, this optimization remains authorized
(whether it is performed is *implementation-defined*).
If the optimization cannot happen, then
the temporary must be materialized and destroyed at the end of full-expression
evaluation. The lifetime of the source object follows
[the established rules](#reloc-src-obj-lifetime).

Note that, if the temporary is materialized and that the source object:

1. *has an eligible relocation or move constructor*: In this case,
    all the owned
    resources of the source object will be *transferred* to the temporary. For this
    reason, we expect that destroying the temporary *will have the same effects
    as* destroying the source object directly.
2. *only has an eligible copy constructor*:
    all the owned resources of the source object will be *copied* to the temporary.
    This is very likely a useless operation.
3. Other cases need not to be considered, as if materialized,
    the `reloc` expression requires the
    source object to declare one of the three constructors.

NOTE: If the source object is a copy-only function parameter, then implementations
are encouraged to emit a warning on discarded reloc statements, as if `reloc`
had the `[[nodiscard]]` attribute. This is because the intent of this statement
is likely to trigger the destruction of the source object (or to trigger the
effect of the destructor, if a move-constructed temporary is materialized and
destroyed). But whether this statement indeed calls a destructor early
(or has any side-effect) for copy-only function parameters is
*implementation-defined*.

To summarize, it means that `reloc obj;` has the following behavior:

- If `obj` is *early destructible* then:
    - The temporary is likely elided, effectively only calling the
        destructor of `obj`.
    - Otherwise a temporary is initialized from the
        source object, and then destructed.
- Otherwise:
    - A temporary is move-or-copy-constructed and then destructed.
    - The source object destruction is deferred until the function returns.

For instance this gives:
```c++
void do_something_01(std::lock_guard<std::mutex> guard)
{
    if (!some_test())
    {
        reloc guard; // ill-formed: no relocation, move or copy constructor
        log("thread ", std::this_thread::get_id(), " failed");
        return;
    }
    bar();
}
void do_something_02(std::mutex& m)
{
    std::lock_guard guard{m};
    if (!some_test())
    {
        reloc guard; // well-formed: direct destructor call is forced to happen
        log("thread ", std::this_thread::get_id(), " failed");
        // ^ logging can happen without holding the lock
        return;
    }
    bar();
    /* guard destructor is called */
}
void do_something_03(std::unique_lock<std::mutex> guard)
{
    if (!some_test())
    {
        reloc guard; /* well-formed: lock is released, either by calling the
            destructor directly, or by constructing a temporary from
            guard (by relocation or move) and destructing it. */
        log("thread ", std::this_thread::get_id(), " failed");
        return;
    }
    bar();
}
void do_something_04(std::mutex& m)
{
    std::unique_lock guard{m};
    if (!some_test())
    {
        reloc guard; /* well-formed: temporary is likely elided regardless of
            do_something_03's ABI, only calling the destructor of guard. */
        log("thread ", std::this_thread::get_id(), " failed");
        return;
    }
    bar();
    /* guard destructor is called */
}
// assuming non_null does not have a relocation constructor
void do_something_05(non_null<shared_ptr<int>> copyOnlyObj)
{
    if (!some_test())
    {
        reloc copyOnlyObj; /* well-formed but its effects are implementation
            defined. Will emit a warning. */
        return;
    }
    bar();
}
```

### Relocation elision ### {#reloc-elision}

Relocation may be elided. This is intended to work in much the same way as the
named return value optimization; for example:

```c+
void f(std::string s);
void g() {
    std::string s;  // may be created in f's argument slot
    f(reloc s);  // relocation may be elided
}
```

More formally, whether performed by relocation, move or copy constructor,
the temporary created by the `reloc` expression may not be materialized if
the implementation can ensure that the source object shares
the same address as the one to be occupied by the target object.

Of course, if relocation elision happens then the rules that impact the
lifetime of the source object still applies.

For instance,
given the following function `void sink(T)`, let us observe the effects of the
`sink(reloc obj)` expression (`obj` being not ref-qualified) across different
ABIs and object lifetime ownerships:

- If `obj` is *early destructible* and `sink` is callee-destroy:
    relocation elision may happen, in which case `obj` occupies the same storage
    as `sink` parameter slot. No new instance is created, and as `sink` is
    callee-destroy, it will take care of the lifetime of `obj`.
- If `obj` is *early destructible* and `sink` is caller-destroy:
    relocation elision may happen, in which case `obj` occupies the same storage
    as `sink` parameter slot. No new instance is created. As `sink` is caller-destroy,
    **the destructor of** `obj` **is implicitly invoked** at the end of the
    full-expression evaluation which invoked `sink`.
- If `obj` is not *early destructible* and `sink` is caller-destroy:
    relocation elision may not happen.
    Right before the invocation of `sink` in the expression `sink(reloc obj)`,
    `obj` is expected to be in a moved-from state. Indeed, because it is not
    *early destructible*, `reloc` will invoke the move constructor, and the
    destruction of `obj` is deferred until control returns to the caller-site.
    But should elision happen, hence bypassing the call to the move constructor,
    then `obj` cannot be left in its moved-from state...
    Hence if `obj` is not *early destructible*, then relocation elision cannot
    happen.
- If `obj` is not *early destructible* and `sink` is callee-destroy: this
    configuration cannot happen in practice.

Likewise, we can observe when elisions are possible given the following
function `void xvsink(T&&)`, invoked through the `xvsink(reloc obj)` expression:

- If `obj` is *early destructible*:
    relocation elision may happen, in which case an rvalue reference of `obj`
    is sent to `xvsink` and the destructor of `obj` is implicitly invoked
    at the end of the full-expression evaluation.
- If `obj` is not *early destructible*:
    Same as above. When `obj` is not *early destructible*, then relocation
    elision loses its interest. A new instance is move-or-copy-constructed from `obj`,
    whose rvalue reference is passed to `xvsink`.


The same kind of elision will happen with an `lvsink(T const&)` function:
elision can only happen when the source object is *early destructible*.

You can read more on relocation elision in the [discussion section](#0-copy).

## Object decomposition ## {#decomposed-object}

This proposal introduces the concept of object decomposition.
Only complete objects can be *decomposed*. An object is *decomposed*
if the `reloc` keyword appears in its definition.
If an object is *decomposed*, then it ceases to exist, but all
its subobjects are considered as complete objects.

**Decomposition is an alternative way to end the lifetime of an object.**

### Declaration and definition ### {#decomposed-object-decl}

#### Object definition #### {#decomposed-obj-def}

In an object definition, the `reloc` keyword can be specified after the
object name:

```c++
T const value reloc = getValue();
```

`reloc` cannot be used in an object declaration or else the program is ill-formed.
Note that `reloc` is not part of the C++ type system. This is emphasized by the
fact that `reloc` can only be specified *after* the object name.

`reloc` can only be used in an object definition
(with `T` the type of the object being defined) if:

- the object is a complete object with automatic storage ;
- the object is a function parameter or a function-local object ;
- `T` is a non-union class-type ;
- `T` is not ref-qualified.

If any of the above conditions is not verified, then the definition is ill-formed.
For instance:

```c++
struct X
{
    std::pair<int, std::string> m reloc; // ill-formed
};

static std::pair<int, std::string> g reloc; // ill-formed

void foo(T const& a reloc) {} // ill-formed (ref-qualified)
void foo(T const* a reloc) {} // ill-formed (not a class-type)
void foo()
{
    T const a[] reloc = { }; // ill-formed (not a class-type)
}

// But
void foo(T const obj reloc) // well-formed if T is a class-type
{
    T obj2 reloc; // well-formed if T is a class-type
}
```

NOTE: We do not foresee any technical limitation that would prevent from
decomposing objects with static storage.
However since the point of the decomposition is to allow
the relocation of the subobjects, and that the relocation of objects with non-automatic
storage is not permitted, we do not see any good reason to authorize it.

If several objects are declared in the same expression, then `reloc` needs to be
repeated for each object which the user wants to decompose:

```c++
T const value reloc = getValue(0), value2 = getValue(1), value3 reloc = getValue(2);
// only "value" and "value3" are decomposed.
T const c, *d reloc; // ill-formed, d is not a class-type
```

#### Function declaration and definition #### {#decomposed-func-def}

Functions parameters may be declared with `reloc`, in which case the parameter
is declared as decomposed.

The `reloc` specifier does not produce a new overload of a function: `f(T)` and
`f(T reloc)` *refer to the same function*.

If a function parameter is declared as decomposed, it needs to be done so
for every visible function declaration and its definition. In our previous
example `f(T)` and `f(T reloc)` refer to the same function, but they have
conflicting declarations, making the program ill-formed.

Indeed, the decomposed parameter may be used in contract expressions (which
must be identical across all visible function declarations), and the fact it's
decomposed has a direct impact on the well-formedness of the contract
expressions that use it.

The parameter decomposition trait may not be attached to a function pointer.
As with contracts, the decomposition trait has no impact on the function type,
and thus no impact on the type of its address.

When a function which decomposes one or more of its parameters is invoked through a
function pointer, the parameter decomposition must still be honored.

```c++
void foobar(T obj reloc);

void foobar(T obj) // ill-formed, not matching its declaration
{}

void foobar2(T obj);
void foobar2(T obj reloc); // ill-formed, not matching previous declaration

struct X
{
    void foo(T obj reloc);
    void bar(T obj);
};

void X::foo(T obj reloc) // well-formed
{}

void X::bar(T obj reloc) // ill-formed, not matching its declaration
{}

static_assert(std::is_same_v<decltype(&X::foo), void (X::*)(T)>); // ok
```

### Decomposition operation ### {#decomposed-state}

When a complete object is defined as *decomposed*, then:

- The object is *initialized* using the existing rules ;
- All its direct bases and non-static data members are now considered as
    distinct *complete* (or [semi-complete](#decompose-virtual-semi-complete)) objects:
    - the decomposition does not odr-use any of the copy, move or relocation
        constructor of the subobjects. In particular, their address remains unchanged ;
    - for all of the virtual and direct bases which have a runtime type information, they
        now point to that of the base class, and no longer point to the type
        of the *decomposed* object. This step is identical as to what happens with
        the runtime type information of base classes once the destructor body
        is executed ;
    - all virtual bases, if any, keep the same address ; all pointers to such
        virtual bases remain unchanged ;
    - the introduced objects (i.e. the subobjects of the *decomposed* object)
        have the same lifetime as the *decomposed* object,
        had it not been *decomposed* ;
    - the introduced objects still inherit from the cv-qualifiers,
        if any, of the *decomposed* object.
- *The lifetime of the* decomposed *object ends*: its destructor is never invoked implicitly.
    All its non-static subobjects, now considered as complete objects, have a
    lifetime of their own.

Note: If not for changing the runtime type of the direct bases that have one,
the decomposition operation would be a no-op.

For instance:
```c++
void foo(std::pair<std::string const, int> kv reloc)
{
    // kv object no longer exists
    // its subobjects are accessible through the decomposed object "kv"
    bar(kv.second, reloc kv.first);
    // kv.second destructor will be invoked implicitly
}

struct B { int _b; };
struct D : B { std::string _d; };
void foo(D const d reloc)
{
    // d.B designates the base of former d object
    static_assert(std::is_same_v<decltype(d.B), B>);
    static_assert(std::is_same_v<decltype(d._d), std::string>);
    bar(reloc d.B); // well-formed
}
```

### Illegal decomposition ### {#ill-formed-decomposition}

The decomposition of an object `src` of type `T` is ill-formed if any of
the following conditions is not satisfied:

- all subobjects of `src` are accessible. They all need to be
    since their destructor will be called if not relocated ;
- `T`'s eligible destructor is not user-provided,
    **or** the call-site has private access privilege to `T`.

These requirements ensure that we can only decompose (a) compound types,
where subobjects have no dependencies with one another (like an `std::pair`),
or (b) types that we are in control of (because of the private access).

An additional requirement applies if the object to decompose is a function
parameter. In that case, `T` must have an eligible relocation, move or
copy constructor. This extra requirement is to make object decomposition
available for function parameters [regardless of the ABI](#decompose-value-param).

### Decomposed objects in expressions ### {#decomposed-obj-expr}

A decomposed object is not an object.
You should think of the decomposed object
as if it were a special namespace holding all its former subobjects.
The decomposed object can be used in expressions as a proxy to access its former
subobjects.

The *id-expression* naming the decomposed object does not yield a value!
(unless it is used in an [unevaluated operand](#decomposed-unevaluated)).

A decomposed object is used in expressions:

- to access a former [data member](#decomposed-data-member) ;
- to access a former [base](#decomposed-direct-base) ;
- to call a [static member function](#decomposed-static-member-func) ;
- to access a former data member through
    a constant-evaluated [pointer to data member](#decomposed-data-member-ptr) ;
- as an [unevaluated operand](#decomposed-unevaluated)
    of `sizeof`, `typeid` or `decltype`.

NOTE: In particular, non-static member functions cannot be called from a decomposed object.
Such expressions are ill-formed.

#### Accessing a data member of a decomposed object #### {#decomposed-data-member}

The data member of a decomposed object can be accessed through the expression:

*decomposed-object* . *data-member*

Such expressions are identical to the matching expression had the object not
been decomposed:

- it yields exactly the same result (same type, same value category) ;
- if the data member is not a direct data member of the decomposed object type,
    then base classes are looked-up for a matching data member. If there are no
    matching data members, several matching data members, or the matching
    data member is inaccessible, then the expression is ill-formed.
- The expression returns a complete object if the data member is a direct data
    member of the decomposed object.
    Otherwise the returned object cannot be relocated
    without decomposing its owner class first.
- The *data-member* expression may not contain disambiguation expressions.


For instance:
```c++
struct B
{
    int _b_pub;
    int _common;
private:
    int _b_priv;
};
struct C
{
    int _common;
};
struct D : public B, public C
{
    int _d;
};

void foo(D d reloc)
{
    d._d = d._b_pub; // valid
    d._d = d._common; // ill-formed, d._common is ambiguous
    d._d = d.C::_common; // ill-formed, disambiguation is not possible
    d._d = d.C._common; // valid, see next section
    d._d = d._b_priv; // ill-formed, _b_priv is private in the context
}
```

NOTE: Static analysis will detect if the accessed data member is still valid,
meaning whether it has already been relocated, or if non direct, whether
it belongs to a base class that has been relocated.

#### Accessing a direct base class of a decomposed object #### {#decomposed-direct-base}

Any base class (even virtual and non-direct) of a decomposed object can be
accessed through the expression:

*decomposed-object* . *base-class-name*

If the base class is not a direct base class of the decomposed object type,
then all non-direct base classes are looked-up. If there are no matching base
classes, several matching base classes, or the matching base class is
inaccessible, then the expression is ill-formed.

The expression returns a complete object only if the base class is a direct
base of the decomposed object. Otherwise the returned object
cannot be relocated without decomposing all
intermediary classes on the inheritance path.

Such expressions allow to access the base class as a data member, as if a data
member named after the class had been declared in the decomposed object,
sharing the same access specifier as that of its inheritance, not ref-qualified,
and with no *cv-qualifiers*.

You might think such expressions are identical to the following expression,
had the object not been decomposed:

`static_cast<B cv &>(obj)`

with `B` being the base class to access, and *cv* the *cv-qualifiers* that
apply to the decomposed object. One notable difference though, is that the
type of the `static_cast` expression is ref-qualified, while the type
of *decomposed-object* . *base-class-name* expression is not.

For instance:
```c++
struct A { int _a; };
struct B : public A { int _common; };
struct V { int _v; };
struct C : public virtual V { int _common; };
struct D : public B, public C
{
    int _d;
};

void foo(D d reloc, D const d_const reloc)
{
    std::println(d.C._common); // valid
    std::println(d.B._common); // valid
    std::println(d.V._v); // valid
    std::println(d.A._a); // valid, d._a is also okay

    static_assert(std::is_same_v<B, decltype(d.B)>);
    static_assert(std::is_same_v<B&, decltype((d.B))>);

    static_assert(std::is_same_v<B, decltype(d_const.B)>);
    static_assert(std::is_same_v<B const&, decltype((d_const.B))>);
}
```

NOTE: Static analysis will detect if the accessed base is still valid,
meaning whether it has already been relocated, or if non direct, whether
it belongs to a base class that has been relocated.

#### Addressing static members from the decomposed object #### {#decomposed-static-member-func}

Static data members and static member functions can be accessed
from a decomposed object as if the object had not been decomposed, reusing
today's syntax.

```c++
struct D
{
    static void func();
    static int _data;
};

void foo(D d reloc)
{
    d.func(); // well-formed
    std::invoke(d.func); // well-formed
    auto i = d._data; // well-formed
}
```

#### Accessing a data member of a decomposed object through pointer #### {#decomposed-data-member-ptr}

A data member of a decomposed object can be accessed by a constant-evaluated
pointer to data member through the expression:

*decomposed-object* . * *pointer-to-data-member*

If the pointer to the data member is not constant-evaluated, then the expression
is ill-formed. Had the pointer been allowed not to be constant-evaluated, then
it would have been difficult for static analysis to know if the pointed data member
is still valid (i.e. whether it has been relocated), and more importantly
whether the pointed data member is a direct data member or one that belongs to
a base class.

Again, such expressions are identical to the matching expression had the object
not been decomposed.

For instance:
```c++
struct B
{
    int _b_pub;
    int _common;
private:
    int _b_priv;
};
struct C
{
    int _common;
};
struct D : public B, public C
{
    int _d;
};

void illformed(D d reloc, int D::* data)
{
    d.*data = d._d; // ill-formed, data is not constant-evaluated
}
consteval void valid1(D d reloc, int D::* data)
{
    d.*data = d._d; // valid
}
template <int D::* Ptr>
void valid2(D d reloc)
{
    d.*Ptr = d._d; // valid
}
void valid3(D d reloc)
{
    constexpr int D::* a = &D::_d;
    d.*a = d._d; // valid
}
```

#### Decomposed object as an unevaluated operand #### {#decomposed-unevaluated}

The *id-expression* designing a decomposed object may be used as operand
of the operators `sizeof`, `typeid` and `decltype`.
The three operators yield the same result as if the object had not been
decomposed, with just a few specificities:

- If `obj` is the decomposed object, then `typeid(obj)` is equivalent to
    `typeid(decltype(obj))` (the RTTI if any is not inspected) ;
- As `(obj)` is an ill-formed expression if `obj` is a decomposed object,
    `decltype((obj))` is also ill-formed.
- `decltype(obj.member_function())` is also ill-formed.

#### Other expressions with a decomposed objects #### {#decomposed-expr-others}

Any expression involving a decomposed object that is not covered in the
subsections above **is ill-formed**.

In particular, with `obj` being a decomposed object, the evaluations of the
following expressions are ill-formed:

- `(obj)`: `obj` is not an object, the expression does not produce a value.
- `foo(obj)`: same as above.
- `(obj).m` (with `m` being a data member): same as above.
- `(obj, a).m` (with `a` being an object of same type): `obj` expression does
    not produce a value, it cannot be used as operand in the comma operator.
- `(a, obj).m`: same as above.
- `auto x = (obj, a)`: same as above.

This is a direct consequence of the `obj` expression not yielding a value.

### Decomposed subobjects in reloc expressions ### {#decomposed-reloc}

The *direct* subobjects of a decomposed object can be used as the operand of `reloc`
expressions. As such objects are complete objects, the `reloc`
expression has the same effect as described in the
[reloc operator](#reloc-operator) section.

Advisement: The only exception to the above is if the `reloc` operand is a
virtual base of a decomposed object. Virtual bases cannot be safely relocated,
and hence their relocation is ill-formed (see [below](#decompose-virtual-bases)).

For instance:
```c++
struct A { int _a; };
struct V { int _v; };
struct B : public A, public virtual V { int _b; };
struct C : public B
{
    int _c;
};

void foo(C obj reloc)
{
    auto c = reloc obj._c; // valid
    // auto b = reloc obj._b; //< ill-formed, _b is not a direct data member of C
    // auto a = reloc obj.A; //< ill-formed, A is not a direct base of C
    auto b = reloc obj.B; // valid
    // auto b2 = obj._b; //< ill-formed, obj._b belongs to B base which has been relocated
    // auto v = reloc obj.V; //< ill-formed, V is a virtual base of C
    auto v = std::move(obj.V); // valid
}
```

### Decomposition and virtual bases ### {#decompose-virtual-bases}

The current proposal does not support the relocation of virtual bases.
Virtual bases can hardly be relocate-only types, and by extension,
can hardly have relocate-only data members.

#### Problem description #### {#decompose-virtual-bases-problem}

Let's consider the following snippet:

```c++
struct A { int _a; };
struct V { int _v; };
struct B : public virtual V { int _b; };
struct C : public B { int _c; };
struct D : public A, public B, public C
{
    int _d;
};

void foo(D obj reloc)
{
    auto c = reloc obj.C; // #1
    auto b = reloc obj.B; // #2
}
```

Now let's see what happens if relocating from a virtual base were allowed.
At #1, `C`'s relocation constructor will initialize
its own `V` base, using relocation semantics. This leaves the `V` base of `obj.C`
in a destructed state. At #2, `B`'s relocation constructor will attempt the
same, except that the `V` base of `obj.B` (which points to the same object as
the `V` base of `obj.C`) is already destructed.
Alternatively,
#1 may throw and stack unwinding will invoke the destructor of `V` which is
likewise already destructed.

You might think that this issue arises from decomposition. In #1 and #2, we ask
to create a new `V` instance twice. But surely it should work in the scope of a
relocation constructor right? Because then only one `V` instance is created. But
then consider the following snippet, with an amended `B`:

```c++
struct B : public virtual V
{
    B(B rhs reloc) : V(std::move(rhs.V)), _b(reloc rhs._b)
    {
        std::cout << rhs._v << std::endl; // #3
    }

private:
    int _b;
};
```

Now the default relocation constructor of `D` will construct `V`, then invoke `B`
relocation constructor. If `D` initializes `V` with relocation semantics,
then `B` relocation constructor will access uninitialized memory at #3!

C++ is already a language with many pitfalls ; one common source of errors is
dangling references, and should virtual bases be allowed to be relocated, then
it would create even more of them...
We propose a twofold solution:

- Virtual bases cannot be relocated ;
- We introduce the notion of [semi-complete](#decompose-virtual-semi-complete) objects.

#### Virtual bases cannot be relocated #### {#decompose-virtual-no-reloc}

Virtual bases are not allowed to be the operand of the `reloc` operator
(otherwise the `reloc` expression is ill-formed).

Instead virtual bases can only be moved-from or copied. This alone guarantees
that no relocation constructor will attempt to relocate a virtual base
(the default implementation moves virtual bases).

```c++
// Given D definition in the above section
void foo(D obj reloc)
{
    auto v = reloc obj.V; // ill-formed
    auto c = reloc obj.C; // #1: okay, the V base of obj.C
    auto b = reloc obj.B; // #2: okay, the V base of obj.B
}
```

At #1 and #2, the new rule ensures that the virtual base is not relocated,
but it states nothing of the lifetime of the virtual base. This is where
[semi-complete](#decompose-virtual-semi-complete) objects come in handy.

#### Semi-complete objects #### {#decompose-virtual-semi-complete}

We introduce the notion of *semi-complete* objects. A *semi-complete* object is a
direct base of a decomposed object, and which inherits (maybe indirectly) of at
least one virtual base.

```c++
// Given D definition in the above section
void foo(D obj reloc)
{
    // obj.B and obj.C are semi-complete objects
    // obj.A, obj.V, and obj._d are complete objects.
}
```

*semi-complete* objects do not own their virtual bases. This means that:

- when they are destroyed, they do not invoke the destructor of their virtual
    bases ;
- when they are passed as parameters of a relocation constructor, it
    will not implicitly invoke the destructor of their virtual
    bases.
    (Using relocation semantics on the virtual bases is also forbidden,
    as [seen above](#decompose-virtual-no-reloc)) ;
- no relocation, move or copy elision can happen from a *semi-complete* object.
    Whether an object is *semi-complete* is established by a static analysis,
    which remains local to the scope of the object.

```c++
void foo(D obj reloc)
{
    auto c reloc = reloc obj.C; // #1
}

B bar(D obj reloc)
{
    return obj.B; // #2
}
```

Expressions such as #1 may simply, thanks to relocation elision,
further decompose `obj.C` without actually performing any relocation.
But because `obj.C` is *semi-complete*, then no relocation elision can happen,
and `c` object is initialized
using relocation semantics. `c.V` is move-constructed from the virtual base of
`obj.C`. `c.B` is *semi-complete* (as `obj.B`).

Return value optimization cannot happen in #2 as `obj.B` is *semi-complete*.
This ensures that the returned
value of `bar` is a *complete* object.

#### Discussion on the solution #### {#decompose-virtual-discuss}

With the established rules, the snippet we had becomes safe, at the cost
of no relocation from virtual bases:

```c++
void foo(D obj reloc)
{
    auto c = reloc obj.C; // obj.V is left in a moved-from state
    auto b = reloc obj.B; // obj.V is reused to initialize b
    // obj.V is implicitly destroyed
}
```

We don't believe this to be a blocking limitation as some mitigation remains possible,
through a custom `T T::exchange(T rhs)` function. Such a function is recommended
to be implemented for any relocate-only type (see `std::box::exchange`). See
for instance:

```c++
struct V
{
    std::box<int> _v; // V is now relocate-only

    V exchange(V rhs reloc)
    {
        return {_v.exchange(reloc rhs._v)};
    }
};
struct B : public virtual V
{
    B(B src reloc) : V{src.V.exchange({})}, _b{reloc src._b} {}

    int _b;
};
struct C : public B
{
    C(C src reloc) : V{src.V.exchange({})}, B{reloc src.B}, _c{reloc src._c} {}

    int _c;
};

struct D : public A, public B, public C
{
    D(D src reloc) : V{src.V.exchange({})}, A{reloc src.A},
        B{reloc src.B}, C{reloc src.C}, _d{reloc src._d} {}

    int _d;
};
```

### Getting the address of an object after its decomposition ### {#decompose-obj-addr}

Sometimes retrieving the address of a decomposed object is needed.
For instance this may be useful in relocation constructors, to adjust some
external objects:

```c++
T::T(T src reloc) : _d{reloc src._d}
{
    gRegistry.erase(/* src address */);
    gRegistry.insert(this);
}
```

We propose the following expression `obj . this` to return the address the object
occupied prior to its decomposition as a *prvalue*, with type `void cv *`,
*cv* being the cv-qualifiers that applied to `obj`.

- Since the expression is a prvalue, it cannot be assigned to a new address and
    its own address cannot be taken (`obj.this = &x` and `&obj.this` are ill-formed).
    Note that `this` today is already a prvalue.
- The return type is a void pointer. As the object has ceased to exist, the
    returned pointer cannot be a pointer of the former object type.

Hence our example becomes:

```c++
T::T(T src reloc) : _d{reloc src._d}
{
    gRegistry.erase(src.this);
    gRegistry.insert(this);
}
```

And we have:

```c++
{
    T obj reloc = {};
    static_assert(std::is_same_v<void*, decltype(obj.this)>);
}
{
    T const obj reloc = {};
    static_assert(std::is_same_v<void const*, decltype(obj.this)>);
}
```

### Decomposition of a lambda closure type ### {#decompose-lambda}

Lambda objects can be decomposed with the rules defined above.
The decomposition can only happen from the lambda body and not from outside
the lambda, as if its captures had private access.

All the lambda captures are accessible through the decomposed lambda.
In practice only the objects captured by value can be relocated.
The objects captured by reference being ref-qualified, if they are used as
 a `reloc` operand, they will then merely be forwarded.

The following is well-formed:

```c++
box<int> ptr;
auto const getPtr = [p = reloc ptr](this auto self reloc)
{
    foo(reloc self.p);
};
(reloc getPtr)();
```
But this is ill-formed:
```c++
box<int> ptr;
auto const getPtr reloc = [p = reloc ptr](this auto self reloc)
//                ^ ill-formed decomposition
{
    foo(reloc self.p);
};
auto ptr2 = reloc getPtr.p;
```

### Implicit decomposition of temporaries ### {#implicit-decomposition}

Temporary objects can be implicitly decomposed to allow relocations in the following
scenarios (but non exclusively):

```c++
struct D : public B {};
D getD();
std::pair<std::string, std::string> getPair();

B b = getD(); /* under conditions, equivalent to:
    auto d reloc = getD(); B b = reloc d.B; */
std::string s = getPair().first; /* under conditions, equivalent to:
    auto pair reloc = getPair(); std::string s = reloc pair.first; */
```

Inspired by &bs[;[class.temporary](https://eel.is/c++draft/class.temporary#6.sentence-2)]
we add a clause to when a subobject of a temporary is to be converted to a
prvalue (for instance because passed to the relocation constructor or relocation
assignment operator).

In all the expressions listed in [6](https://eel.is/c++draft/class.temporary#6.sentence-2),
the resulting expression can be converted to a prvalue with *implicit decomposition*:

- In [6.3](https://eel.is/c++draft/class.temporary#6.3), the C-array case,
    the array decomposition is always legal and each of its elements are considered
    as complete objects (their destructor will be implicitly invoked).
    It's only if that array element is relocated or further implicitly decomposed
    that its destructor will not
    be implicitly invoked (for that element only).
- In [6.5](https://eel.is/c++draft/class.temporary#6.5), the pointer to data
    member case, **the pointer to data member must be constant-evaluated**.
    The language will identify which data member is pointed at.
    It's only if that data member is relocated or further implicitly decomposed
    that its destructor will not be implicitly invoked.
    The pointer must be constant-evaluated, as it is otherwise impossible
    to know at compile-time whether it refers to a direct data member or
    to one of a base class (in which case further implicit decompositions will
    happen). This information is required at compile-time to assess the
    well-formedness of the expression.
    If the pointer is not constant evaluated, then no implicit decomposition
    can happen.

The *implicit decomposition* materializes the temporary complete object.
Further implicitly decompositions occur for each data member access
(or array element access for array-types) or cast to a base class,
to extract the desired subobject.

If any *implicit decomposition* is illegal, then it does not cause the program
to be ill-formed, the expression simply cannot be converted
to a prvalue (i.e. remains an xvalue).
All the incurred decompositions must be legal, as defined
below, or else the *implicit decomposition* is
illegal. In particular, if any virtual base is to be decomposed,
then the *implicit decomposition* is illegal.

#### Ill-formed implicit decomposition #### {#implicit-decomposition-ill-formed}

An *implicit decomposition* of an object of type `T` is ill-formed
if any of the following conditions is not satisfied:

- all subobjects of `T` are accessible. They all need to be
    since their destructor will be called if not relocated ;
- `T`'s eligible destructor is not user-provided.
    Unlike in the explicit decomposition, private access privilege does not
    allow to bypass this check.

#### Examples #### {#implicit-decomposition-examples}

```c++
class T
{
public:
    std::string _pub;
private:
    std::string _priv;
};
class U
{
public:
    std::string _pub[5];
};

T getT();
U getU();

std::pair<std::string, std::string> getPair();


std::string a = getT()._pub; /* implicit decomposition is ill-formed
    because T has a private member, move constructor is used */
std::string b = getU()._pub[2]; /* implicit decomposition is well-formed
    std::string's relocation constructor is used; _pub[0] to _pub[4] but _pub[2]
    are destroyed */
std::string c = getPair().second; /* implicit decomposition is well-formed;
    std::string's relocation constructor is used */

class V
{
public:
    V();
    ~V();
    static std::string GetDefaultName()
    {
        return V()._name; /* implicit decomposition is ill-formed
            because T has a user-defined destructor.
            V()._name remains an xvalue.
            Note that this code is valid today, and should
            implicit decomposition be legal in that case (because of the private
            access privilege), then the expression
            would not have the same effect as ~V would not be invoked. */
    }
private:
    std::string _name;

};

```

### Decomposition of value parameters ### {#decompose-value-param}

As we have seen, decomposition ends the lifetime of the source object.
The reader may be concerned that this may break existing ABIs, should the source
object be a function parameter passed by value.
However we do not foresee any extra breakage other than what the support for
the `reloc` operator does.

Indeed if the decomposed object is not *early destructible*, then the function
may silently relocate its argument to some local storage before the function
body is entered. *This is a putative
workaround and is not part of the proposal.* Nonetheless it is made possible
in the [object decomposition requirements](#ill-formed-decomposition).

For instance:

<table>
<tr><th>User-written code</th><th>Putative equivalent generated code</th></tr>
<tr>
<td valign="top"><pre lang="cpp">
void foo01(T obj reloc)
{
    // ...
    if (test())
    {
        sink(reloc obj.m);
    }
    // ...
}
</pre></td>
<td valign="top"><pre lang="cpp">
void foo01(T __obj)
{
    auto obj reloc{reloc __obj};
    // ...
    if (test())
    {
        sink(reloc obj.m);
    }
    // ...
}
</pre></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td valign="top"><pre lang="cpp">
void foo02(T obj reloc) try
{
    // ...
    if (test())
    {
        sink(reloc obj.m);
    }
    // ...
}
catch (...) {}
</pre></td>
<td valign="top"><pre lang="cpp">
void foo02(T __obj) try
{
    auto obj reloc{reloc __obj};
    // ...
    if (test())
    {
        sink(reloc obj.m);
    }
    // ...
}
catch (...) {}
</pre></td>
</tr>
</table>

### Decomposition in constexpr ### {#dot-reloc-constexpr}

Object decomposition can be used in constexpr functions and
constant-evaluated expressions.

### Examples ### {#decomposed-object-examples}

#### Nominal case #### {#decomposed-object-ex-nominal}

One of the main practical uses of object decomposition is for compound types, such as
`std::pair`. Note that this code would not be feasible without relocation and
without decomposition.

```c++
void bar(string, box<int>);

void foo(pair<string const, box<int>> p reloc)
{
    bar(reloc p.first, reloc p.second);
}
```

#### Custom extractor #### {#decomposed-object-ex-extractor}

```c++
class T
{
private:
    string const _key;
    box<int> _val;

public:
    // [...]
    box<int> getVal(this T self reloc)
    {
        return self._val;
        // note that here the destructor of self is not called.
        // however every non relocated data member is still destructed
        // so self._key.~string() is called at function exit.
    }
};
```

This code may be invoked in the following situations:
```c++
// with: T getT();
//       T src;

auto val1 = getT().getVal();
auto val2 = (reloc src).getVal();
```

#### Putative std::unique_ptr::release #### {#decomposed-object-putative-release}

```c++
template <class T, class Deleter>
class unique_ptr
{
// ...
public:
    T* release(this T self reloc)
    {
        return reloc self._ptr;
    }
};
```

Because of the decomposition, the destructor of `self` is not called. This code
safely releases the guarded pointer. Note that this `release` function
is not part of the proposal.

## Ill-formed reuse of relocated objects ## {#ill-formed-reuse}

### Variable state tracking ### {#variable-state-tracking}

This section describes the static analysis that is required to prevent
reuses of a relocated object.

The `reloc` operator changes the state of its operand, imposing
restrictions of how the variable can be used in the next expressions.

Let us formally introduce the new states a local variable can have:

- *alive* (**A**): this is the default state. As of today in C++, all variables are in this
    state.
- *relocated* (**R**): the variable was passed as operand to the `reloc` operator.
- *alive-or-relocated* (**A-R**): the variable may be *relocated* or *alive*. For instance
    the variable will have this state at the end of an if statement,
    if the variable was *relocated* in one path and remained *alive* in the other.

Those states are evaluated *at compile-time*, and their primary use is to verify
the well-formedness of expressions.

The state of a given variable (including function parameters) is evaluated as follows:

- The variable starts in the **A** state.
- The state of the variable is tracked by *examining* potentially-evaluated expressions,
    from one expression to the next, following the evaluation order.
    - If two or more expressions have an unspecified evaluation order with
        one another, then
        expressions containing a `reloc` operator with the tracked variable as
        operand are examined first (in any order).
    - Expressions that are part of [unevaluated operands](https://eel.is/c++draft/expr.context#1)
        are not examined.
    - Expressions that are part of a
        [discarded statement](https://eel.is/c++draft/stmt.if#def:discarded_statement)
        of a `constexpr if` statement are not examined.

- The *id-expression* designating the tracked variable is ill-formed
    if the expression is examined and the variable's state is not **A**.
    - If the *id-expression* designates a non-direct subobject of a decomposed
        object, then its state refers to the state of the direct base that contains it.
    - If the *id-expression* is used as operand of the `reloc` operator,
        then the variable transitions to the **R** state after the
        `reloc` expression examination.

- If the code path forks (for instance because of an if statement), then all the
    different code branches need to be examined.
- If two or more code paths rejoin, then the resulting state of the variable
    is the **OR**-combination of the state it had in all the joined code branches.
- On a code jump:

    - if the code jumps before the variable declaration or after the variable
        enclosing scope, then the state analysis for this code branch stops here
        for this variable.
    - otherwise the state of the variable after the jump is the **OR**-combination
        of the state it had before the jump, and the state it would have had at the
        destination code location without taking the jump.
        If the destination code location cannot be reached without jump, then
        the jump does not change the state the variable.
        The state of the variable prior to the jump is memorized for this code jump.
    - if a same code jump needs to be taken an extra time, then the state of the variable
        is compared to the one previously memorized. If they are identical then
        the state analysis for this code branch stops here for this variable.
        Otherwise the new state prior the jump is updated and the jump re-examined.

- In a try block, if the variable was declared before the block: at the end of
    an expression that may throw,
    a code jump to each catch block may be examined.
    This code jump is necessary:

    - if the state of the variable has changed prior to when the last jumps
        to the catch blocks were made.
    - or if no last jumps to catch blocks were made, then compare the state
        the variable had when entering the try block against the current state
        and perform the jumps if they differ.

- This state analysis stops until code reaches the natural end of scope of the
    variable.

NOTE: We don't expect this analysis to be costly. It is only necessary in functions
for the variables that are relocated. If there are none,
then this analysis is not needed.

### reloc in unevaluated operands ### {#unevaluated-operands}

The `reloc` operator can be used in
[unevaluated operands](https://eel.is/c++draft/expr.context#1).
Such expressions are ignored by the code analysis described
[above](#variable-state-tracking).
If `reloc`  is used in an unevaluated operands,
then (a) their operands are always considered to have the *alive* state,
and (b) the state of their operands do not change.

For instance:
```c++
void foo(T x)
{
    decltype(reloc x) y = reloc x; // well-formed
    decltype((reloc x, (reloc x).m)) z = y.m; // well-formed
}
```

### Examples ### {#reloc-dot-reloc-ill-formed-showcase}

Consider the following examples:

```cpp
void reloc_showcase_01()
{
    const T var = getT();
    bar(reloc var);
    if (sometest(var)) // ill-formed: state of 'var' is relocated
        do_smth(var); // ill-formed: state of 'var' is relocated
}
```
`var` cannot be reused after the `reloc` call.
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_02()
{
    const T var;
    {
        const T var;
        bar(reloc var);
        do_smth(var); // #1 ill-formed: state of 'var' is relocated
        {
            const T var;
            do_smth(var); // #2 OK
        }
        do_smth(var); // #3 ill-formed: state of 'var' is relocated
    }
    do_smth(var); // #4 OK
}
```
At #2 and #4, the calls to `do_smth(var)` are allowed because the name `var`
does not resolve to the relocated object.
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_03()
{
    const T var = getT();
    if (sometest(var))
        bar(reloc var); // #1, var state is relocated
    else
        do_smth(var); // #2, var state is alive
    // var state is alive-or-relocated
}
```
At #2 `do_smth(var)` is allowed because `var` state is not affected by the
`reloc` call at #1 (they occur in different code branches).
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_04()
{
    const T var = getT();
    if (sometest(var))
        bar(reloc var); // #1
    else
        do_smth(var); // OK
    // [...]
    do_smth_else(var); // #2, ill-formed: state of 'var' is A-R
}
```
At #2, `do_smth_else(var)` is an error because `var` is mentioned after the
`reloc` call at #1.
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_05()
{
    const T var = getT();
    if (sometest(var))
        bar(reloc var);
    else
        do_smth(reloc var); // OK
    // var state is relocated
}
```
Both `reloc` are okay as they happen on different code paths.
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_06()
{
    const T var = getT();
    bool relocated = false;
    if (sometest(var))
    {
        bar(reloc var);
        relocated = true;
    }
    else
        do_smth(var); // OK
    // [...]
    if (!relocated)
        do_smth_else(var); // ill-formed: state of 'var' is A-R
}
```
It does not matter that the developer tried to do the safe thing with the
`relocated` variable. The code-path analysis disregards run-time values and
explores all branches of an `if` statement.
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_07()
{
    constexpr bool relocated_v = my_can_relocate<T>{}();
    const T var = getT();
    if constexpr(relocated_v)
    {
        bar(reloc var);
    }
    else
        do_smth(var); // OK
    // [...]
    // var state is R if relocated_v is true, A otherwise
    if constexpr(!relocated_v)
        do_smth_else(var); // OK
}
```
The above example is safe because of the use of `if constexpr`.
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_08()
{
    const T var = getT();
    if (sometest(var))
    {
        bar(reloc var);
        return;
    }
    do_smth(var); // OK
}
```
This example is also safe thanks to the `return` statement right after the
`reloc` instruction, which prevents from running `do_smth(var);`.
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_09()
{
    const T var = getT();
    for (int i = 0; i != 10; ++i)
        do_smth(reloc var); // ill-formed: state of 'var' is A-R (on second iteration)
}
```
This is ill-formed as each iteration reuses `var` which is declared before
the loop body.

In the code path analysis:

1.  At the first loop iteration, the variable gains the *relocated* state.
2.  The loop increment is examined, and it does not change the state of `var`.
3.  The loop condition is examined, and it does not change the state of `var`.
4.  The code jump is examined for the first time, memorizing the state of `var`
    at that moment (*relocated*).
5.  The new state of `var` is now **A-R**.
6.  `do_smth(reloc var)` is examined, and `reloc var` is ill-formed as `var`
    is not in the *alive* state.

```cpp
void reloc_showcase_10()
{
    const T var = getT();
    for (int i = 0; i != 10; ++i)
    {
        if (i == 9)
            do_smth(reloc var); // ill-formed: state of 'var' is A-R (on second iteration)
        else
            do_smth(var); // ill-formed: state of 'var' is A-R (on second iteration)
    }
}
```
This is ill-formed for the same reason as above.
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_11()
{
    const T var = getT();
    for (int i = 0; i != 10; ++i)
    {
        if (i == 9) {
            do_smth(reloc var); // OK, var state is relocated
            break;
        }
        else
            do_smth(var); // OK, var state is alive
    }
    // var state is A-R
}
```
Adding the break statement right after the `reloc` call makes the code snippet well-formed.
Indeed the `break` statement forces the loop exit, which implies that the
conditional jump at the end of loop (that may start the next iteration) is no
longer part of the code path that follows the `reloc` instruction.
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_12()
{
    for (int i = 0; i != 10; ++i)
    {
        const T var = getT();
        do_smth(reloc var); // OK
    }
}
```
`var` is local to the for-loop body, so `reloc` is well-formed here.
The code path analysis for this variable does not require several loop iterations,
as the variable scope ends before the code jump leading to the next iteration
is evaluated.

```cpp
void reloc_showcase_13()
{
    const T var = getT();
from: // #1
    if (sometest(var)) // #2, ill-formed: var state is A-R after the jump
    {
        do_smth(var); // #3, ill-formed: var state is A-R after the jump
        return;
    }
    else
    {
        do_smth(reloc var); // #4, ill-formed: var state is A-R after the jump
    }
    // #5
    goto from;
}
```
Because of #4, the state of `var` at #5 is **R**. The goto statement is executed
and we are back at #1. `var` state is then the OR combination of the **A** state
and the **R** state, which is **A-R**.
Then the expressions #2, #3 and #4 are ill-formed because `var` is not in the
**A** state.
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_14()
{
    const T var = getT();
from:
    if (sometest(var)) // var state is alive
    {
        do_smth(var); // var state is alive
        goto from;
    }
    else
    {
        do_smth(reloc var);
    }
}
```
In this scenario `goto` is placed in a way that does not trigger the reuse of relocated `var`.
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_15()
{
    union { T x; U y; };
    x = getT();
    if (sometest(x))
    {
        do_smth(reloc x);
        x = getT(); // well-formed
    }
    else
    {
        reloc x; // destroy x
        y = getU();
        do_smth_else(reloc y); // well-formed
        x = getT(); // well-formed
    }
    do_smth_else(reloc y); // well-formed
}
```
`reloc` does not prevent from reusing the names of anonymous union members.
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_16()
{
    T x;
    if (sometest(x))
    {
        do_smth(reloc x);
    }
    else
    {
        auto const y reloc = reloc x;
        do_smth_else(reloc y.m); // well-formed
        // equivalent to: do_smth_else((reloc x).m);
    }
    do_another_thing(x); // ill-formed: state is now R
}
```
`do_another_thing(x)` is ill-formed as `x` is relocated.
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_17()
{
    T x;
    if (sometest(x) || sometest(reloc x)) // well-formed: x state is A-R
    {
        do_smth(x); // ill-formed: x state is A-R
    }
    else if (othertest(x)) // ill-formed: x state is A-R
    {
        foo();
    }
}
```
```cpp
void reloc_showcase_18()
{
    // unspecified evaluation order
    { T x; auto y = reloc x + reloc x; } // ill-formed
    { T x; auto y = x + reloc x; } // ill-formed
    { T x; auto y = reloc x + x; } // ill-formed
    { T x; auto y = x[reloc x]; } // ill-formed

    // sequenced evaluation order
    { T x; auto y = x, reloc x; } // well-formed
    { T x; auto y = reloc x, x; } // ill-formed
    { T x; auto y = x or reloc x; } // well-formed
    { T x; auto y = reloc x or x; } // ill-formed

    // unspecified evaluation order with ternary
    { T x; foo(x, reloc x); } // ill-formed
    { T x; foo(x, test() ? reloc x : x); } // ill-formed
    { T x; foo(test() ? reloc x : x); } // well-formed
}
```

If two or more expressions have an unspecified evaluation order,
then to determine their well-formedness
we examine the expressions containing `reloc` first, and
examine the rest after (in any order).

```cpp
void reloc_showcase_19()
{
    T x = getT();
    try
    {
        foo();
        bar(reloc x);
    }
    catch (std::exception const& excp)
    {
        std::cerr << excp.what() << " for " << x << std::endl;
        // ill-formed: x state is alive or relocated
    }
}
```

### Conditional destruction ### {#conditional-reloc}

NOTE: This section only applies to variables that are not ref-qualified.

The variable state analysis described [here](#variable-state-tracking)
may be used to know what kind of cleanup is necessary at scope exit
(i.e. if the end of scope is reached or an exception is emitted).

When this happens, if the variable is in **A-R** state, and that its type is
not trivial, then
implementations must track *at runtime* the actual state of the variable
(**A** or **R**)
and process its destruction accordingly:

- If the variable was the operand in an evaluated `reloc` expression, then
    the variable may need to be destructed following the rules described [here](#reloc-src-obj-lifetime).
- Otherwise the variable must be destructed by its destructor following normal
    rules.

NOTE: If the variable was the operand of a `reloc` expression,
    but that expression was *not evaluated*, then
    *its lifetime is not affected* and the variable must still be destroyed
    normally.

How this runtime tracking is done is
*implementation-defined*, although it will probably take the form of extra flags
in the function stack.

For instance:
```c++
void foo()
{
    T obj = /* ... */;
    if (some_test())
        bar(reloc obj); // obj state is relocated
    else
        handle_error(); // obj state is alive
    live_on(); // obj state is alive-or-relocated
}
```

After the call to `live_on()`, or should `live_on` throw, `obj` destructor
is only implicitly invoked if `reloc obj` was not evaluated. Implementations may simply turn on
a flag right before the evaluation of the `reloc` expression.

Concerns have been expressed that extra flags would violate the "don't pay for
what you don't use" principle. We acknowledge this concern, but feel that the
user is getting at least something out of this. Alternatives would be for the source
object to be destroyed implicitly at the opening or closing brace of the `else`,
for the code to be ill-formed unless the source object is relocated within the
`else`, or for the code to be ill-formed unconditionally. Note that in each of these
cases, the user can recover our preferred behavior in library, using `std::optional`
to add the tracking flags that in our proposal would be provided by the language.

## Relocation constructor ## {#reloc-ctor}

Now that we have introduced the `reloc` operator and object decomposition, we
introduce the relocation constructor, which is built upon those new concepts.
As relocation happens from prvalues,
the constructor takes a prvalue as parameter.
The relocation constructor must also relocate the resources of the source object
and will rely on *object decomposition* for that purpose. Its simplest spelling
is `T(T reloc)`; the full general form is given [below](#reloc-ctor-declaration).

This signature was picked as it completes the C++ tripartite value system.
The copy constructor creates a new instance from an lvalue, the move constructor
from an xvalue, and then the relocation constructor from a prvalue.

Note: a further benefit of this syntax is that it is currently ill-formed
<a href="http://wg21.link/class.copy.ctor#5">[class.copy.ctor]/5</a>, and
thus available for extension.

A point of confusion may be that the syntax implies an infinite regress: the
parameter must be constructed, which requires a prior call to the relocation
constructor, and so on. This is not the case; if the source object was previously
the operand of the `reloc` operator, it was transformed into a prvalue
immediately before entering the relocation constructor, and the parameter of the
relocation constructor is that same prvalue. (If the source object was already a
prvalue, there is no issue; the parameter is that prvalue.)

An attractive intuition is that the parameter aliases the source object in the
same way as a reference or a structured binding declaration.
However, this is misleading; the lifetime of a source object glvalue has already
ended and so use of a pointer or reference referring to the source object has
undefined behavior, except as provided by [basic.life] and [class.cdtor].

Note: this behavior matches that for the destructor of a class type; see
<a href="https://wg21.link/basic.life#1.4">[basic.life]</a> paragraph 1.

This intuition is only useful in so far as the ABI for a relocation
constructor prvalue parameter is likely to be the same as that for a copy or move
constructor parameter, since the prvalue parameter may have the same storage
location as a previously existing glvalue.

Note: it does not matter that the ABI for the relocation constructor parameter
differs from that for a prvalue parameter in normal functions, since it is not
possible to take the address of a constructor.

The role of the relocation constructor is to construct a new instance by *destructively
stealing* the resources from the source object. Unlike the move
constructor, the relocation constructor needs not to leave the source object in
valid state.

### Declaration ### {#reloc-ctor-declaration}

The relocation constructor can be declared (implicitly or explicitly), defaulted
and deleted like any other constructor.

As noted above, `T(T reloc)` is used in this paper as shorthand.
Formally, the relocation constructor must take a decomposed source object of same type
(modulo cv-qualifiers) as first parameter.
Extra, defaulted parameters [are authorized](#reloc-ctor-additional-params).
Hence its general form is `T(T cv src reloc, [...])`, with `cv` the cv-qualifiers
of `src`, and where `[...]` denotes only parameters with default initializers.
In particular, the signature `T(T)` (without `reloc`)
remains illegal as of today.

A class-type that provides a relocation constructor has some impact on the program
ABI. See the [ABI section](#abi).

#### Implicit declaration #### {#reloc-ctor-declaration-implicit}

If no user-defined relocation constructors are provided for a class-type `T`,
and all of the following is true:

* there are no user-declared copy constructors ;
* there are no user-declared copy assignment operators ;
* there are no user-declared move constructors ;
* there are no user-declared move assignment operators ;
* there are no user-declared relocation assignment operators ;
* there is no user-declared destructor ;
* there are no base class of `T` which has a virtual destructor but no
    [virtual slicing function](#virtual-slicing).

Then the compiler will declare a relocation constructor as a non-explicit inline
public member of its class with the signature `T::T(T reloc)`.

#### Deleted implicitly-declared or defaulted relocation constructor #### {#reloc-ctor-declaration-deleted}

The implicitly-declared or defaulted relocation constructor for class `T` is defined as deleted:

- if `T` has subobjects that explicitly declare a deleted relocation constructor ;
- or `T` has subobjects with missing relocation and move constructors (i.e.
    that are deleted, inaccessible, or ambiguous).
- or `T` has subobjects with deleted or unaccessible destructor.

As for move constructors, a defaulted relocation constructor that is deleted is
ignored by overload resolution.

Note: this means that a class with an explictly deleted relocation constructor
will still be relocated if necessary, but through the move (or copy) constructor
and destructor.

#### Exception specification #### {#reloc-ctor-except}

Although it is not advised, the relocation constructor is allowed to terminate
by throwing an exception (just like the destructor). As for any other function,
a noexcept specification can be specified on the relocation constructor.

A relocation constructor that is implicitly declared or defaulted on its
first declaration has a `noexcept(true)` noexcept specification unless any
constructor or destructor of a subobject that the default definition would
call is *potentially-throwing*.

A non-defaulted user-declared relocation constructor with unspecified noexcept
specification is *potentially-throwing*.

#### Trivial relocation #### {#trivial-relocation}

A class-type that has virtual bases or is polymorphic cannot be trivially
relocatable, because of how those features are sometimes implemented.

**About virtual inheritance**:
A common implementation for virtual inheritance is to store in the memory layout
of the derived class a pointer to the virtual base. This is incompatible with
trivial relocation as the pointer needs then to be adjusted to the new memory
location. Another possible implementation would be to store an offset to the
virtual base location, which would make it trivially relocatable.

**About polymorphic types**:
You might think that polymorphic types can be trivially relocatable, as they
only contain a pointer to a virtual table. However this is not true on some
implementations. Quoting [[D3780R0]]'s explanation:

> [...] On some architectures/ABIs (such as `arm64e`) these [vtable] pointers
> are *authenticated*, meaning that some bits of the pointer value are used to
> store a cryptographic signature. This signature gets verified when one accesses
> the virtual table pointer (for instance by calling a virtual function).
>
> One of the inputs of the signature is target address of the pointer (that is,
> the address of the virtual table). Another input, which is crucial to this
> story, is the address of the virtual table pointer itself.
>
> **This means that, on those architectures, relocating a polymorphic object by
> using a simple byte copy would produce an object whose virtual table pointer
> would fail to authenticate.**

**As a consequence**,
a relocation constructor of a class type `T` is *trivial* if it is not user-provided and
if:
* `T` has no virtual base classes, and
* `T` is not a polymorphic type, and
* for each direct base class and direct non-static data member of class type or array thereof,
    the relocation operation (which may be a relocation constructor or
    synthesized from copy/move constructor plus destructor) selected
    to relocate that base or member is trivial.

Note that implementations are allowed to benefit from the trivial relocation
optimization (i.e. identical to a `memcpy` call) if the class-type is not trivially
relocatable because of virtual inheritance or polymorphism (should their
implementation of such class traits be compatible with trivial relocation).

C++26 defines **trivial relocatability**. We amend this definition to account
for the relocation constructor.
A *trivially relocatable class* is one which:
* has a trivial, eligible relocation constructor, or ;
* does not have a relocation constructor (including one that is deleted),
    and is *trivially relocatable* according to C++26.

Note: *eligible* is defined in
<a href="http://wg21.link/special#def:special_member_function,eligible">[special]</a>.

Scalar types, trivially relocatable class types, and arrays and cv-qualified versions
thereof are *trivially relocatable*.

Advisement: We also tighten the definition of "trivial class" (and thus "trivial") to
require that the class in question be trivially relocatable as well as trivially
copyable. This is to ensure that if the user wants code to be called on relocation,
the library does not bypass said code by, say, using `memmove`.

#### Interaction with C++26 memberwise_trivially_relocatable #### {#trivial-and-memberwise_trivially_relocatable}

This proposal provides its own way to define trivial relocation, while C++26
also provides the `memberwise_trivially_relocatable` trait.
We do not see it as a blocking point:

- `memberwise_trivially_relocatable` can be specified by mistake causing
    undefined behaviors. On the other hand, having the trivial relocation
    trait deduced by whether the relocation constructor is trivial is a safer
    approach, which does not make room for human errors ;
- `memberwise_trivially_relocatable` only affects objects that live in
    dynamic storage, while the relocation constructor is also available for
    objects with automatic storage. They answer different needs.

We propose `memberwise_trivially_relocatable` to have no effect if the class-type
declares a user-provided relocation constructor (i.e. the constructor is
declared and not defaulted). Under this condition, the class-type cannot be
considered as trivially relocatable.

This behavior follows the existing logic
`memberwise_trivially_relocatable` has with regards to virtual bases:
it's not an error if the two are combined, `memberwise_trivially_relocatable`
is merely ignored.

Note that `memberwise_trivially_relocatable` is not equivalent to defining
a defaulted relocation constructor, as the latter will have an impact on the ABI.
Hence in the following snippet:

```c++
struct T memberwise_trivially_relocatable
{
    T(T const&);
    T(T&&);
};
void foo(T x)
{
    sink(reloc x); // #1
}
```
Whether relocation happens at `#1` depends on the implementation, that is on whether
`x` is *early destructible*. If relocation happens, then `x` can be trivially
relocated, as if by `memcpy`.

If this proposal is accepted, we expect `memberwise_trivially_relocatable`
to vanish over time in favor of the more natural relocation constructor
default declaration.

### Definition ### {#reloc-ctor-definition}

#### Decomposition of the source object #### {#reloc-ctor-dtor-def}

As its signature suggests, the relocation constructor decomposes its source object.
Refer to [the above section](#decomposed-state) to see how decomposition unrolls.

#### Virtual bases #### {#reloc-dtor-virtual-bases}

Virtual bases have a special treatment in constructors and destructors:
they are only constructed and destructed by the class-type of the complete object,
and not by its base classes.
The relocation constructor has the same behavior.
Hence it must be passed with the same extra information
that is passed today to any constructor and to the destructor:
(a) whether it is in charge of the construction of the virtual bases, and
(b) whether it is in charge of the destruction of the virtual bases of the source object.

It follows that the relocation constructor will construct the virtual
bases of the target object (respectively destroy them from the source object)
only if the extra information *(a)* (respectively *(b)*) states so.

For instance:
```c++
class B
{
    B(B src reloc);
    // [...]
};
class C : public virtual B
{
    C(C src reloc);
    // [...]
};
class D : public C
{
    D(D src reloc);
    // [...]
};

void foo(C);

void bar()
{
    D const d reloc = getD();
    foo(reloc d.C); // #1
}
```

Here, at #1 when `bar` calls the `C`'s relocation constructor to build the
parameter of `foo`:

- `C(C)` is in charge of constructing the virtual base `B` ;
- `C(C)` is **not** in charge of destroying the virtual base `B` of its parameter.
    `src.~B()` is not called by the relocation constructor ;
- The default implementation of `C(C)` will move-construct the virtual base ;
- `foo` gets a `C` object owning its virtual base.

After #1:

- `d.B` still points to valid data, albeit that it was likely moved-from
    in `C(C)` ;
- `d.B` destructor will be implicitly invoked, as it will be for all the data
    members of `d.D` ;

#### Default definition #### {#reloc-ctor-default-definition}

The default relocation constructor implementation for a class-type `T` depends
on `T`'s type traits.

If `T` is trivially relocatable then the relocation constructor
effectively (ignoring padding) performs
a `memcpy` over its entire memory layout.

Otherwise the constructor implementation first decomposes
the source object, and then performs
memberwise relocations. In the relocation constructor `T(T src reloc)`,
for each subobject `s` (of type `S`) of `T`, in declaration order:

- if `S` is a virtual base, then `S` is move-or-copy-constructed as if by:
    `S{std::move(src.S)}` ;
- otherwise we construct `s` (or `S` for direct base classes)
    by calling its own relocation, move or copy
    constructor, as if by: `s{reloc src.s}` (or `S{reloc src.S}`);

#### User-provided definition #### {#reloc-ctor-user-definition}

Although the default definition should cover almost all cases, users can provide
their own definition of the relocation constructor. Doing so is relatively
straightforward thanks to the object decomposition feature.
Custom relocation constructors are important to cover non-trivial relocation
patterns in real code bases (for example self-referencing fixups and similar
invariant adjustments), though the default implementation should suffice for
most classes.

Consider the following examples:

```c++
struct T
{
    std::string _a, _b, _c;

    // Dummy example to explain all the steps of the relocation constructor.
    T(T src reloc) :
        _a{std::move(src._a)}, _c{reloc src._c} {} /*
        1. T::_a is constructed using the move constructor.
        2. T::_b is default constructed.
        3. T::_c is constructed using std::string's relocation
           constructor, from src._c.
        4. src._b and src._a are destructed (in that order)
           when the constructor body exits.
    */
};

struct U
{
    std::string _a;

    ~U();

    // Second dummy example.
    U(U src reloc) {} /*
        1. U::_a is default-constructed.
        2. src._a is destructed.
    */
};

// Motivating example
template <class T>
class BinaryTree
{
public:
    // works also if T is relocate-only
    BinaryTree(BinaryTree src reloc) noexcept : _root{reloc src._root}
    {
        /* fixup references */
        if (_root._left)
            _root._left->_parent = &_root;
        if (_root._right)
            _root._right->_parent = &_root;
    }
private:
    struct BTNode {
        BTNode* _parent = nullptr;
        BTNode* _left   = nullptr;
        BTNode* _right  = nullptr;
        T _value;
    };
    BTNode _root;
};
```

#### Delegating relocation constructor #### {#reloc-ctor-delegate}

We do not support writing a delegating relocation constructor. This is hardly
possible because of the decomposition of the source object.

We do not consider this as a blocking issue.
[Additional default parameters](#reloc-ctor-additional-params)
are supported with the relocation constructor. And constructor delegation
may still happen with an extra layer:

```c++
class T
{
    struct Impl
    {
        // [...] all data members useful to T go there instead.
    };

public:
    T(T src reloc) : T{reloc src._impl, {}} {}
    //                ^ delegating constructor

private:
    T(Impl src, Helper h) : _impl{reloc src} {}

    Impl _impl;

};
```

#### Additional parameters #### {#reloc-ctor-additional-params}

As with copy and move constructors, it is permissible to add additional parameters
to a relocation constructor, on condition they have a default initializer.

One case where this can be of use is if the user needs space to store information
and/or resources
for the duration of the relocation constructor, for a contrived example:

```c++
class T
{
public:
    class Helper {
    public:
        Helper() = default;
        ~Helper() { delete p; }
    private:
        friend T;
        int* p;
    };

    T(T src reloc, Helper storage = {}) noexcept(false)
        : _p(storage.p = std::exchange(src._p, nullptr))
        , _q{reloc src._q}
        , _r{reloc src._r}
    {
        storage.p = nullptr;
    }

    ~T() {
        delete _p;
    }

private:
    int* _p;
    RelocateOnly _q;
    ThrowingRelocate _r;
};
```

In the above, `T::_p` does not manage its own lifetime,
but the presence of `T::_r` means that `T::T(T reloc)` is not noexcept so we need to
release its resources if an exception is thrown during relocation.

### Invocation ### {#reloc-ctor-invoke}

The relocation constructor is invoked *as necessary* to relocate a prvalue
from one storage location to another. Use of the `reloc` operator does not
guarantee that a relocation constructor (if present) will be called,
since it may be elided
if the compiler can arrange that the source glvalue was constructed at the
appropriate address.

In particular, code of the form `auto x = reloc y;` is *highly* likely to be a
no-op, simply renaming an existing object. This is however likely to find use
for "sealing" objects with complex initialization, replacing the idiom of
immediately-invoked function expressions (IIFEs, [[IIFE]]):

<table>
<tr><th>Before</th><th>After</th></tr>
<tr>
<td valign="top"><pre lang="cpp">
T const x = std::invoke([&]
{
    T x;
    x.modify(y, z);
    return x;
});
</pre></td>
<td valign="top"><pre lang="cpp">
T x_mut;
x_mut.modify(y, z);
T const x = reloc x_mut;
</pre></td>
</tr></table>

Or, consider:

```c++
C f(int i) {
    C c1, c2;
    if (i == 0)
        [[likely]] return reloc c1;  // #1
    else if (i == 1)
        [[likely]] return c1;  // #2
    else
        [[unlikely]] return c2;  // #3
}
```

At `#1` the `reloc` is largely redundant; the end-of-life optimization means
the compiler is entitled to treat `c1` as a prvalue anyway, as in `#2`. Indeed,
the likelihood annotation encourages the compiler to construct `c1` in the
return slot, such that both `#1` and `#2` are a no-op. It is only `#3` that is
likely to invoke the relocation constructor.

### Virtual slicing function ### {#virtual-slicing}

Although relocation and polymorphism are unlikely to be mixed together, there
are still situations where it may happen. Consider the following scenario:

```c++
class D : public B { std::string _str; };

B* b = new D;

B sliced = std::reloc_and_reclaim(b);
// reloc_and_reclaim relocates the given address to
// its return value and deallocates the memory.
```

The proposed relocation design must ensure this will not to cause any
object slicing. In order to address this loophole, we propose a new virtual
slicing function. [std::reloc_and_uninitialize and std::reloc_and_reclaim](#std-reloc_and_uninitialize)
must then use it, if present, to prevent object slicing.

#### Declaration #### {#virtual-slicing-decl}

A class-type that explicitly declares a relocation constructor and has a virtual destructor,
then implicitly declares a virtual slicing function, with the same access
specifier as that of the destructor:

- The relocation constructor must be explicitly declared and not deleted.
- The virtual destructor may be declared in the class-type, or inherited from
    a base class.

If a class-type inherits from a base type that provides a virtual slicing function,
then it also triggers the declaration of the slicing function with the same
access specifier as that of the destructor.

The virtual slicing function cannot be user-declared and cannot be invoked
by the user. It acts as a hidden
virtual function that makes the link between the relocation world and the
polymorphism world.

```c++
struct Type1 {}; //< no virtual slicing function

struct Type2
{
    Type2(Type2 const&) = default;
    Type2(Type2&&) = default;
    Type2(Type2 reloc) = default;
    ~Type2() = default;
    // no virtual slicing function
};

struct Type3
{
    virtual ~Type3() = default;
    // no virtual slicing function, relocation constructor is not implicitly declared
    // because of the destructor declaration
};

struct Type4
{
    Type4(Type4 reloc) = default;
    virtual ~Type4() = default;
    // declares a virtual slicing function
};

struct Type5 : public Type3
{
    // no virtual slicing function.
    // the relocation constructor is not implicitly declared,
    // as the inherited virtual destructor prevents its implicit declaration
};

struct Type6 : public Type3
{
    Type6(Type6 reloc) = default;
    // declares a virtual slicing function
};

struct Type7
{
    Type7(Type7 reloc) = default;
    // no virtual slicing function because no virtual destructor
};

struct Type8 : public Type7
{
    virtual ~Type8() = default;
    // no virtual slicing function because no explicit relocation constructor
    // (relocation constructor is not implicitly declared)
};

struct Type9 : public Type8
{
    Type9(Type9 reloc) = default;
    // declares a virtual slicing function
};
```

As you can see, the ABI break introduced by the new virtual function can only
be opt-in: if no relocation constructor is explicitly declared, then there are
no ABI breaks. See also the discussion about the [virtual slicing function](#virtual-slicing-func-discussion).

#### Definition #### {#virtual-slicing-def}

Let `T` be a class-type with a virtual slicing function.
The aim of the slicing function is to decompose `T`, destroy all the unwanted parts
but the desired base class, and to relocate that base class to a new storage
space.

The parameters of the slicing function are *implementation-defined*. At minimum
there must be:

- the object to slice, which is the `this` parameter ;
- the target type of the object to construct, which we will call `U`.
    `U` is by construction, either `T` or a (maybe non-direct) base class of `T`
    which provides a slicing function.
    `U` may be a template parameter (in which case the slicing function is
    a template function, possibly causing multiple vtable entries), or a
    type information supplied at run-time.
- the address where it is to be constructed, which we will call `u`.

The slicing function handles the destruction of its `this` object. Upon exit
(normally or through an exception), the `this` object is destroyed.

A possible implementation of the virtual slicing function is:

1.  If `U` is the same as `T`, then relocates `this` to `u`. This is done by
    invoking the relocation constructor, move constructor or copy
    constructor. If the relocation constructor was not invoked, then the
    destructor of `this` must be invoked.
1.  Otherwise let `V` be the direct base of `T` which is on the inheritance path
    from `T` to `U` (`V` may be `U`):

    1. Decompose `this`. Then recursively invoke the `V`'s slicing function
        on `V` subobject, forwarding the `U` and `u` parameters.
    1. Upon exit, the destructors of all non-relocated subobjects
        of `src` are implicitly invoked.

The slicing function is defined in the same translation units of the destructors.
If the destructor is inline, then so is the slicing function.

#### Ill-formed definition #### {#virtual-slicing-def-ill-formed}

A virtual slicing function definition of a class-type `T` is ill-formed if:

- **the destructor of** `T` **is user-provided** ;
- or `T` has no eligible relocation, move and copy constructor ;
- or `T` has no eligible destructor and relocation constructor ;
- or any of the direct base of `T`, which declares a virtual
    slicing function, has a non-accessible slicing function ;
- or if any of the subobject of `T` has an inaccessible destructor.

All points but the first merely ensure that all the functions which the slicing
function may call are invocable from that context.

The virtual slicing function may only perform *implicit* decompositions,
which is enforced by the fact that the destructor must not be user-provided.
This design decision is motivated by the following example:

```c++
struct B
{
    B(B reloc) = default;
    virtual ~B() = default; //< triggers virtual slicing function declaration
};
struct D : public B
{
public:
    D() : _ptr{new T} {}
    D(D reloc) = default;
    ~D() override { delete _ptr; }
private:
    T* _ptr;
};
```

The slicing function that slices a `D` object to `B` would decompose its source
`D` object, hence bypassing the call to `D`'s destructor ...
causing a memory leak. This is why the slicing function definition
is ill-formed if the destructor is user-provided.

A possible fixup to the above example is to wrap `_ptr` to an RAII object, such as
`std::unique_ptr`. Then the destructor can be defaulted, and the slicing function
will call the `unique_ptr`'s destructor.

## Relocation assignment operator ## {#reloc-assign-operator}

We further introduce the relocation assignment operator.
Its signature shall be:
`T& T::operator=(T [reloc])`, the parameter decomposition being optional.
If the parameter is decomposed, the function is then called the *decomposing
relocation assignment operator*.
Such non-decomposing operators may already be defined in existing codebases,
but the proposed changes will not interfere with them.

Sometimes we also make mentions to the *prvalue-assignment operator*.
It refers to the same function,
but further indicates that this function existed prior to the proposal.

### Declaration ### {#reloc-assign-declaration}

The relocation assignment operator becomes a special member function. As such,
declaring one breaks the Rule of Zero, which was not the case previously.
A user-declared relocation assignment operator will prevent an implicit
declaration of the relocation constructor.

The relocation assignment operator may be implicitly declared, and may be
defaulted or deleted.

Only the *decomposing* version may be defaulted
(`T::operator=(T) = default;` is an ill-formed definition).

#### Implicit declaration #### {#reloc-assign-operator-implicit}

If a class-type follows the Rule of Zero, then the compiler will declare
an inline public decomposing relocation assignment operator.

#### Deleted implicitly-declared or defaulted relocation assignment operator #### {#reloc-assign-declaration-deleted}

The implicitly-declared or defaulted relocation assignment operator for class `T` is defined as deleted:

- if `T` has subobjects that have an implicitly or explicitly deleted relocation assignment operator ;
- or `T` has subobjects that have inaccessible relocation, move or copy assignment operators ;
- or `T` has subobjects with deleted or unaccessible destructor.

A defaulted relocation assignment operator that is deleted is ignored by overload
resolution.

#### Exception specification #### {#reloc-assign-op-noexcept}

Just like the relocation constructor, the relocation assignment operator is
allowed to terminate  by throwing an exception.

A relocation assignment operator that is implicitly declared or defaulted on its first
declaration has a `noexcept(true)` noexcept specification unless any assignment operator
or destructor of a subobject that the default definition would call is
*potentially-throwing*.

A non-defaulted user-declared relocation constructor with unspecified noexcept
specification is *potentially-throwing*.

### Relocation assignment operator parameter relocation elision ### {#aliased-reloc-assign}

As with the relocation constructor, it is desirable that the parameter should be
the source object *converted to* a prvalue, and not a temporary prvalue relocated
*from* the source object. This is particularly critical for
the default definition of the operator, which
(as you might suspect) performs memberwise calls to other relocation assignment operators.
Without elision, that would imply recursive relocation of each subobject, down to
their smallest unbreakable parts (incurring O(N<sup>2</sup>) byte copies, *N* being the size in
bytes of the source object).

However, this poses a problem since it is possible to take the address of a
relocation assignment operator, yielding a pointer (or reference) with (typical)
signature `T& (T::)(T)`, implying that the source object must occupy a parameter slot,
which may not find it possible to have the same storage address as the source object,
and/or which the caller may expect to destroy (see [[#abi]]).

Nevertheless, **we mandate elision if the class-type (possibly implicitly)
declares a non-deleted relocation constructor,
or a decomposing relocation assignment operator (possibly defaulted)**.
The proposed elision is performed in such a way as to avoid ABI break (more on that on the [ABI
section](#prvalue-assign-op-abi)).

If elision is mandated, then the assignment operator declaration actually
declares two member functions:

- the non-eliding one, which takes its input parameter by value.
    If no optimizations allow otherwise, this is the function that will be
    invoked when user-code calls the
    assignment operator. It is the prvalue-assignment operator as we know it
    today ;
- the eliding one, which takes its input parameter as if by reference, and has the
    same return type as the non-eliding one. The eliding
    function has no identifier and does not participate in overload resolution.
    Users cannot take its address.
    The eliding operator is forced to have callee-destroy ABI: the operator
    is in charge of the lifetime of the source object.

The definition of the assignment operator (which is user-provided or
defaulted) will serve as the definition of the eliding operator.

The non-eliding operator definition is generated by the compiler, and merely wraps
the call to the eliding one:

- If the source object passed to the non-eliding operator is *early destructible*,
    then the operator:
    1.  Calls the eliding operator, passing the source object as if by reference.
    2.  It forwards as return value whatever the eliding operator returns.
- Otherwise then the operator:
    1.  Creates a local copy of the source object, using move or copy constructor.
    2.  Calls the eliding operator, passing that copy by reference.
    3.  It forwards as return value whatever the eliding operator returns.
    4.  It does not call the destructor of that local copy.

NOTE: If the implementation can guarantee that the relocation assignment operator parameter
is passed by address and is *early destructible*, then the non-eliding version is
not needed.

If the address of the assignment operator is queried, then the address of the
non-eliding version is returned. If the assignment operator is virtual, then only
the non-eliding version is considered to be `virtual` and is added to the vtable entry.

### Definition ### {#reloc-assign-definition}

#### Default definition #### {#reloc-assign-default-definition}

As you would expect, the default definition merely delegates to the relocation
assignment operator of all its subobjects.
In `T`'s default assignment operator, the source object `src` of type `T` is
decomposed, and for all its subobjects `s` of type `S`:

- if `S` is a virtual base, then `S` is move-or-copy-assigned as if by:
    `static_cast<S&>(*this) = std::move(src.S);` ;
- otherwise we construct `s` (or `S`) by calling its own relocation, move or copy
    assignment operator, as if by: `s = reloc src.s;`
    (or `static_cast<S&>(*this) = reloc src.S;` for base classes);

Thanks to the relocation-assignment operator special eliding,
no copies or relocations of the source subobjects are performed.

Also note that this implementation has a strong exception safety, which
guarantees to leave the object in a valid state if left by throwning an exception.

#### Possible user definitions #### {#reloc-assign-user-definition}

The default definition is enough *if the class destructor is also defaulted*.
If the destructor body performs some resource management
(meaning that the resource management is not handled by a subobject's destructor)
then the same kind of resource management is likely expected to take place
in the relocation assignment operator, requiring a user-provided implementation.

This section enumerates some notable implementations.

**destroy-and-construct**

One may be tempted by the infamous *destroy-and-construct* idiom.
It is both convenient (no need to duplicate destructor code and write relocation
code for each subobject) and efficient
on paper. But if not used with caution, it can lead to serious undefined behaviors
(likely program crashes).

```c++
T& T::operator=(T src) noexcept
{
    std::destroy_at(this);
    return *std::construct_at(this, reloc src);
}
```

This function has a poor exception-safety.
Should any of the destructor or the
relocation constructor throws, the function would exit leaving `this` in a
destructed state (uninitialized memory)... hence the `noexcept`.

An exception-safe version is possible, requiring more work:

```c++
T& T::operator=(T src) requires std::is_default_constructible_v<T>
{
    std::experimental::scope_fail _{[this] { new (this) T; }};
    std::destroy_at(this);
    return *std::construct_at(this, reloc src);
}
```

Despite the improved exception safety, this pattern is still dangerous when
mixed with inheritance. Consider the following code:

```c++
struct B
{
    virtual ~B() = default;
    B& operator=(B src); //< using destroy-and-construct idiom
};
struct D : B { std::string _str; };

foo(B& obj) { obj = B{}; }

void bar()
{
    D obj;
    foo(obj);
    // obj._str is now destructed and obj runtime-type info points to B's!
}
```

*destroy-and-construct* is only safe with a non-throwing destructor and
relocation constructor, and for non-polymorphic types. *This implementation
is not recommended*.

**relocate-and-swap**

```c++
T& T::operator=(T src)
{
    using std::swap;
    swap(*this, src);
    return *this;
}
```

This implementation has two advantages: it delegates the resource cleanup of
`this` to the destructor of `src` (no duplicate code) and is exception-safe.
Note however that it is not the most efficient approach.

**Cleanup and memberwise relocation**

```c++
T& T::operator=(T src)
{
    cleanup();
    auto src_d reloc = reloc src; // inplace decomposition
    static_cast<B&>(*this) = reloc src_d.B; // relocate base
    this->_a = reloc src_d._a;
    return *this;
}
```

This implementation is preferred over the other two suggested above. `cleanup`
will perform the resource management performed in the destructor body (if the
destructor is defaulted, then so should be the relocation assignment operator).

Note that the decomposition of the source parameter happens after `cleanup`.
If `cleanup` terminates by throwing an exception, then `src` must be destroyed
(its own `cleanup` must be called by its destructor). Had it been decomposed
from the start, its destructor call would have been bypassed, causing a memory
leak in the exception scenario.

Alternatively, a simpler version is possible, should we not want to be bothered
by exceptions:

```c++
T& T::operator=(T src reloc) noexcept
{
    cleanup();
    static_cast<B&>(*this) = reloc src.B; // relocate base
    this->_a = reloc src._a;
    return *this;
}
```

**Putative std::unique_ptr implementation**

Let's illustrate the *cleanup and memberwise relocation* pattern by a concrete
case. This section discusses the possible implementations of the relocation
assignment operator for `unique_ptr`.

The simpler implementation is:

```c++
unique_ptr<T,D>& unique_ptr<T,D>::operator=(unique_ptr<T,D> rhs reloc) noexcept
{
    reset();
    _deleter = reloc rhs._deleter;
    _ptr = rhs._ptr;
    return *this;
}
```

The implementation is straightforward, we simply deallocate the resources and
perform memberwise relocations. The decomposition of `rhs` ensures its destructor
is not run. This implementation assumes that the deleter object is nothrow
relocatable.

Note that, had `reset()` been allowed to throw (`reset()` is `noexcept`), the
implementation would have been slightly more evolved:

```c++
unique_ptr<T,D>& unique_ptr<T,D>::operator=(unique_ptr<T,D> rhs)
{
    reset(); //< can throw, then rhs destructor is called
    auto rhs_members reloc = reloc rhs;
    _deleter = reloc rhs_members._deleter;
    _ptr = rhs_members._ptr;
    return *this;
}
```

In this version, the decomposition happens at a later stage (note that the
relocation caused by the decomposition will be elided). This is to ensure that
any exception thrown in the resource destruction sequence (`reset()`) will
trigger the proper destruction of `rhs`.

**relocate-and-exchange**

```c++
T T::exchange(T new_value);

T& T::operator=(T src)
{
    exchange(reloc src);
    return *this;
}
```

This implementation relies on an `exchange` function that sets the object
to a new value (`src`) and returns its former value. Note that such a function
is encouraged to be implemented in all relocate-only types.

This implementation is preferred over the *relocate-and-swap* pattern. It
benefits from the same advantages and has less relocation operations involved
than what's needed for a swap.

**Putative std::box implementation**

Likewise, let's see a concrete implementation of *relocate-and-exchange* pattern
with `std::box`. For this section, let's consider a more realistic `std:box<T,A>`
with an allocator object.

A simple implementation will only need the *cleanup and memberwise relocation* pattern:

```c++
box<T,A>& box<T,A>::operator=(box<T,A> rhs reloc) noexcept
{
    _alloc.deallocate(_ptr);
    _alloc = reloc rhs._alloc;
    _ptr = rhs._ptr;
    return *this;
}
```

This is straightforward, just as the noexcept `unique_ptr` implementation seen
above. Likewise, an exception-safe version requires more work but is still
possible, thanks to the *relocate-and-exchange* pattern:

```c++
box<T,A> box<T,A>::exchange(box<T,A> other reloc)
requires std::is_nothrow_relocation_constructible_v<A>
{
    box const prev{_ptr, std::reloc_and_uninitialize(std::addressof(_alloc))};
    new (std::addressof(_alloc)) A{reloc other._alloc};
    _ptr = other._ptr;
    return prev;
}

box<T,A>& box<T,A>::operator=(box<T,A> rhs)
{
    exchange(reloc rhs);
    return *this;
}
```

This implementation has a strong exception safety: should an exception be thrown,
the `box` would still be in a valid state. Indeed, an exception may only be
thrown by `T`'s destructor, and this is only called after the `box` is set a new
valid state (i.e. `rhs`), when the object returned by `exchange` is destroyed.

### Invocation ### {#reloc-assign-invoke}

```c++
T x, y;
x = reloc y;
```

Every call to the relocation assignment operator follows normal rules.

If the call site detects that an eliding version of the operator is available,
then which version of the operator
is called is implementation-defined.

The nominal case is to call the non-eliding version. The implementation is allowed
to call the eliding version instead, given that the source object is
*early destructible*.

## Overload resolution ## {#overload-resolution}

The current overload resolution rules are not suitable for relocation by prvalue.

Indeed, consider the following scenario:
```c++
void bar(T&&); // same issue with: bar(T const&);
void bar(T);

void foo(T val)
{
    bar(reloc val); /* ambiguous call using today's rules */
}
```

Hence we propose a change in the overload resolution rules to prefer passing by
value for prvalue arguments.

Specifically, we would amend <a href="https://eel.is/c++draft/over.ics.rank#3.2.3">[over.ics.rank]/3.2.3</a> to read:

- neither of S1 and S2 bind a reference to an implicit object parameter of a
    non-static member function declared without a ref-qualifier, and either:
    - S1 binds an lvalue reference to an lvalue, and S2 does not, or:
    - S1 binds an rvalue reference to an xvalue, and S2 does not, or:
    - S1 does not bind a reference, and S2 binds a reference to a prvalue, or:
    - S1 binds an rvalue reference to a prvalue, and S2 binds an lvalue reference [Example:
        ```c++
        int i;
        int f1();
        int&& f2();
        ...
        int g2(const int&);
        int g2(int);
        int g2(int&&);
        int j2 = g2(i); // calls g2(const int&)
        int k2 = g2(f1()); // calls g2(int)
        int l2 = g2(f2()); // calls g2(int&&)
        ...
        ```
        &#45; end example]


### Comparison against P2665R0 ### {#overload-against-P2665R0}

[[P2665R0]] "Allow calling overload sets containing T, const T&" also proposes
to modify the overload resolution rules. It allows overload sets containing
both `T` and `T const&` to exist, and which overload is selected is
implementation-defined. The main motivation is for performance: "small" trivial
types, such as built-in integral types, are best passed by value than by
reference.

In what we propose, overload sets with `T`, `T const&` and `T&&` can also exist.
However, which is picked is (not *early destructible* objects left aside)
specified clearly by the standard
(depending on the value category of the parameter) and
not *implementation-defined*.

The `reloc` operator can be used to turn not-ref-qualified
automatic variables into prvalues, which allows the user to force the right
overload.

The only situation where the overload being selected is implementation-defined,
is when a function parameter passed by value is being relocated. Whether the
relocation constructor is considered in the overload resolution is
implementation-defined, as it depends on the ABI capabilities. Consider:

```c++
void foo(T);
void foo(T&&);

void bar(T value)
{
    foo(reloc value); /* always calls foo(T),
        but how foo's parameter is constructed
        is implementation-defined (reloc, move or copy ctor) */
}
```

In this example, `bar` will always call the `foo(T)` overload, as `reloc value`
is always a prvalue. However, the materialization of this prvalue is
implementation-defined, the relocation constructor being potentially
evicted.

NOTE: This proposal and P2665R0 can co-exist. Even with the updated overload
resolution rules that we propose, compilers may still be allowed to pick the
prvalue overload if they analyse it may bring a performance boost, as P2665R0
suggests.

## Structured decomposition ## {#structured-decomposition}

### Motivation ### {#structured-decomposition-discussion}

`auto [x, y] = foo(); sink(reloc y);` is ill-formed given the rules we
established for `reloc`. `x` and `y` are not complete objects but aliases to
some anonymous object which the language creates behind the scene.

As this proposal aims to provide support for relocate-only types, this support
would be partial, if not impractical, without allowing some form of
relocation from a structured binding.

In our experience, most C++ developers believe that a structured binding is
a complete, separate object, and not a name alias to some subobject. As such
it would feel unnatural for them if they cannot relocate from a structured binding.

We propose "structured decomposition" as an alternative to structured bindings.
The subobjects of the initializer are still bound to the new identifiers,
except that they are now considered as complete objects thanks to the object
decomposition feature.
The new identifiers can then be relocated.

### Structured decomposition declaration ### {#structured-decomposition-declaration}

A structured decomposition declaration is syntactically identical to a structured
binding, with the exception that
no ref-qualifiers are allowed after the type specifier,
and the declared object must have automatic storage.

```c++
T foo();
T const& bar();
T foobar();

// [...]

auto [x, y] = foo(); // matches structured decomposition declaration
auto const [w, z] = bar(); // matches structured decomposition declaration
thread_local auto const [w, z] = bar(); // matches structured bindings declaration
auto&& [a, b] = foobar(); // matches structured bindings declaration
```

The structured decomposition declaration will initialize an anonymous object
(named `e` here) from an *initialization* expression.
`e` is then *implicitly* decomposed, and each of its subobjects is bound to the
newly introduced identifiers.

For that purpose, there are three [initialization and binding protocols](#structured-decomposition-obj-decomposition).

#### Implicit vs explicit decomposition #### {#structured-decomposition-implicit}

The decomposition that happens with structured decomposition is *implicit*:
no `reloc` keyword is required nor allowed to explicitly mark the decomposition.
The implicit decomposition follows its own rules, described [here](#implicit-decomposition).

The difference between implicit and explicit decomposition is that the former
has almost no side effect. Most notably, the implicit decomposition is only legal
if the destructor is defaulted, meaning that the destructor body can safely
be bypassed.

The intent of structured decomposition is merely to allow the relocation of the
identifiers, not to bypass some resource management code enforced by the destructor.
Hence structured decomposition relies on implicit decomposition especially for its
lack of side effects, making it much safer to use.

Alternatively structured decomposition could have been explicit, emphasized by
the `reloc` keyword. But that would have implied added complexity, as developers
would need to be careful of the side effect of the destructor, should they use
structured decomposition from a code location with private access privilege.

```c++
class T
{
    std::box<std::string> _name;
public:
    T();
    ~T();

    static std::box<std::string> GetDefaultName()
    {
        auto [name] reloc = T();
        //   ^ beware that ~T() is no longer invoked...
        return name;
    }
}
```

This kind of gotchas are not possible with implicit decomposition (the above
code would fallback to structured bindings, and the return statement would be
ill-formed). This motivated us to rely on implicit object decomposition
for structured decomposition.

### Structured decomposition protocols ### {#structured-decomposition-obj-decomposition}

As there are three binding protocols for structured bindings, there are likewise
three "structured decomposition" protocols that apply on the *initialization* expression.
If none of those protocols match, then the declaration is considered to be
a structured bindings declaration.

The protocols are tested in the following order:

1. The *array* protocol ;
1. The *get_all* protocol ;
1. The *data members* protocol ;

In what follows, let `S` be the type of the *initialization* expression,
deprived of its reference and cv qualifiers.

#### array protocol #### {#structured-decomposition-array}

For this protocol to apply, all the following conditions must be satisfied:

- `S` must be an array-type of known bound ;
- the number of identifiers must equal the number of array elements ;

If this protocol applies, then `e` is initialized from the *initialization*
expression. The *i*-th identifier is bound to the *i*-th array element of `e`
(yields the same result and value category as the `e[i]` expression,
except that it is now a complete object).

NOTE: This is the only proposed way to decompose an array.

```c++
void foo()
{
    std::string const strs[3] = { /* ... */ };
    // [...]
    auto const [str1, str2, str3] = reloc strs;
    bar(reloc str2);
}
```

#### get_all protocol #### {#structured-decomposition-get_all}

The function `get_all(S)` is looked-up using ADL-lookup. If there is
no match, then this protocol does not apply.

If there is a match, then this function is called using the *initialization*
expression to initialize the function parameter.
The returned type is tested recursively:

1. If *get_all* matches for the returned type, then we reapply it again using
    the returned value as new parameter ;
1. If *data members* protocol matches for the returned type, then it is applied ;
1. If no protocol matches for the returned type, then the expression is
    **ill-formed**. The expression is no longer a candidate for a structured
    bindings declaration as long as one *get_all* protocol matched.

This follows the same recursive logic as `operator->()`.
We recursively call `get_all` as long as the *get_all* protocol applies.
When the recursion ends, we end up with a type which
matches the *data member* protocol, or else the expression is ill-formed.

This protocol does not say how `e` is initialized and how the identifiers are
bound. Those will happen at the end of the recursion by the *data member*
protocol.

NOTE: If there are concerns that `get_all` may clash with existing functions,
then other names may be used, such as `__get_all__` or `__decompose__` or
even `operator[]reloc`.

#### data members protocol #### {#structured-decomposition-dm}

Let `t` be the resulting expression after unrolling the `get_all` recursion
(`t` is the *initialization* expression if the *get_all* protocol was not
applied) and `T` the type `t`,
deprived of its reference and cv qualifiers.

For this protocol to apply, all the following conditions must be satisfied:

- `T` is a non-union class-type ;
- every non-static data member of `T` must be an accessible direct member of
    `T` or of the same base class of `T`.
    Let's call `B` the class containing the data members ;
- the number of identifiers must equal the number of non-static data members of `B` ;
- `B` may not have an anonymous union member ;

If this protocol applies, then `e` is initialized with `t`. If `T` is different
from `B`, then `e` is recursively *implicitly* decomposed until the base class
`B` is reached. All the *implicit* decompositions must be
[legal](#implicit-decomposition-ill-formed) or else the declaration is ill-formed.

Finally, let's call `b` the decomposed object that contains the data members.
The *i*-th identifier is bound to the *i*-th non-static data member of `b`
(it yields the same result and value category as the `b.i` expression).

#### Implementation-defined library support #### {#structured-decomposition-tuple}

`std::tuple`, and `std::array` shall provide their own implementation
of `get_all`. The return type is *implementation-defined* (may rely on compiler
magic, which will return a flat structure containing all the tuple or array
elements as data members).

`std::pair` already complies with the *data member* protocol and does not need a
`get_all` implementation.

This allows us to write things like:
```c++
void bar(T);
void foo(std::vector<T>& v)
{
    /* This code uses a putative vector::pilfer(const_iterator) (not proposed),
     * which removes a vector element at given iterator, and
     * returns a pair with next valid iterator and relocated vector element. */
    auto [val, it] = v.pilfer(v.begin() + 1);
    bar(reloc val); /* can call reloc on val as it is not a structured binding */
}
```

This code works even if `T` is relocate-only.

#### Possible get_all implementations #### {#structured-decomposition-implem}

Thanks to `std::tuple`'s `get_all` we can easily write
a `get_all` implementation for a custom class:

```c++
class MyType
{
public:
    MyType();
    MyType(MyType reloc);

    // Possible implementation:
    auto get_all(this MyType self reloc)
    {
        return std::tuple{_rep, reloc self._name, reloc self._box};
    }

private:
    Repository& _rep;
    std::string _name;
    std::box<int> const _box;

};
```

Advisement: The implementation relies on a putative new constructor for `std::tuple`
(not proposed) which captures the tuple elements by value category,
which allows to relocate objects into a tuple.

Then, in the following snippet:
```c++
MyType tp;
auto [rep, name, box] = reloc tp;
// equivalent to: auto [rep, name, box] = get_all(get_all(reloc tp));
```

`MyType`'s `get_all` returns a tuple. `get_all` is defined for tuples as well, so
it is called again. The second return type won't have a `get_all` defined, hence
the recursion stops and the *data member* protocol is used.

And we have:
```c++
static_assert(std::is_same_v<Repository&, decltype(rep)>);
static_assert(std::is_same_v<std::string, decltype(name)>);
static_assert(std::is_same_v<std::box<int>, decltype(box)>);
```

# Proposed library changes # {#proposed-lib-changes}

NOTE: Adding support for relocation in the STL is another major change.
What we are proposing to add for the moment is the bare minimum to allow
library writers to benefit from relocation. Support for relocation in the STL
will be done in a separate proposal, depending on the reception of the current
proposal.

## Memory header ## {#std-mem-header}

### std::construct_at ### {#std-construct_at}

We propose to add the following overload to `std::construct_at`:

```c++
template<class T>
constexpr T* construct_at( T* p, T src );
```

Which would be equivalent to `::new (p) T{reloc src}`, except that it may be
used in constant expression evaluations.

Note: this overload would be unnecessary if the [[#future-capture-value]]
direction were to be adopted; instead the existing signature should be
altered to use the `decltype(Args)...` placeholder.

### std::reloc_and_uninitialize and std::reloc_and_reclaim ### {#std-reloc_and_uninitialize}

We propose to add the following functions in the `std` namespace in the `memory`
header to perform relocation through a pointer:

```c++
template <class T>
std::remove_cv_t<T> reloc_and_uninitialize(T* src);
template <class T>
std::remove_cv_t<T> reloc_and_reclaim(T* src);
```

Both functions construct a new object by calling either
the virtual slicing function, the relocation constructor, the move constructor
or the copy constructor (in that order),
using `*src` as parameter while ignoring its cv-qualifiers, and return it.
They also ensure the end-of-life of the source object: if the move or copy
constructor is called then the destructor of `*src` is called afterwards.

`reloc_and_uninitialize` leaves the memory that was occupied by `src` in an
destructed state (uninitialized memory).  Even if it leaves with an exception,
then `reloc_and_uninitialize` is guaranteed to leave the source object in a
destructed state.

`reloc_and_reclaim` reclaims the memory by calling the appropriate deallocation
function (class-defined `delete` or global `delete`). If `reloc_and_reclaim`
leaves with an exception, then it is guaranteed to leave the source object in a
destructed state and to have deallocated its memory.

`reloc_and_uninitialize` is intended to be used by library authors to enable relocation
from a memory address. For instance, extracting a value out of an optional just
becomes:

```c++
T optional<T>::extract()
{
    _has_value = false;
    return std::reloc_and_uninitialize(_value_addr());
    // _value_addr() being a private function returning the address of the owned value
}
```

Of course, this function is not intended to be used on local objects. Hopefully
its name should prevent misuses.

```c++
void foo()
{
    const T val;
    bar(std::reloc_and_uninitialize(&val)); /* BAD, val destructor is called at the
        end of its scope while it is already destructed!*/
}
```

`reloc_and_reclaim` is intended to be used by end users and library vendors alike.

```c++
unique_ptr<pair<string, box<T>>> makeStuff();

box<T> getBox()
{
    auto stuff = makeStuff();
    auto [str, relocOnly] = std::reloc_and_reclaim(stuff.release());
    return relocOnly;
    // or the short version:
    // return std::reloc_and_reclaim(makeStuff().release()).second;
}
```

### std::relocate ### {#std-relocate}

C++26 introduced `std::relocate` that allows to relocate a range of contiguous
objects. This function can be only be used if the object is trivially relocatable
or has a non-throwing move constructor.

We propose to amend this function, so that it can also be used if the object
has a non-throwing relocation constructor. The function then attempts
trivial relocation, then fallbacks to relocation, and then to move construction.

## Type traits header ## {#std-type_traits-header}

We propose to introduce the following type traits, which all derive from
`std::true_type` or `std::false_type` accordingly:

As with existing copy/move traits, these relocation traits are behavioral:
they test whether the corresponding construction or assignment expression is
well-formed (and whether it is trivial or non-throwing), not whether a
specific relocation special member function is explicitly declared.

- `std::is_relocation_constructible<T>`: same as `std::is_constructible<T, T>` ;
- `std::is_trivially_relocation_constructible<T>`: same as
    `std::is_trivially_constructible<T, T>` ;
- `std::is_nothrow_relocation_constructible<T>` same as
    `std::is_nothrow_constructible<T, T>` ;
- `std::is_relocation_assignable<T>`: same as `std::is_assignable<T, T>` ;
- `std::is_trivially_relocation_assignable<T>`: same as `std::is_trivially_assignable<T, T>` ;
- `std::is_nothrow_relocation_assignable<T>`: same as `std::is_nothrow_assignable<T, T>` ;
- `std::is_decomposable<T>`: derives from `std::true_type`
    if and only if `std::is_class_v<T>` is true, and `T` decomposition is legal ;
    Derives from `std::false_type` otherwise ;

The existing trait `std::is_trivially_relocatable<T>` shall be amended to take
into account the [updated definition](#trivial-relocation).

For all new type traits, the inline constexpr boolean template variable counterparts
are also proposed. For instance we propose to introduce:
```c++
template< class T >
inline constexpr bool is_relocation_constructible_v = is_relocation_constructible<T>::value;
// along with the rest
```

# Discussions # {#discuss}

## Potential ABI changes ## {#abi}

As noted above ([[#early-destructible]]), some platforms have a *caller-destroy* ABI
where the *calling* function expects to destroy nontrivial parameters passed by
value. This poses a problem for functions that wish to relocate from such
parameters, and a potential ABI break.

### relocate-only function parameters ### {#callee-destroy-abi}

In the [case study](#reloc-with-function-param) of how `reloc` should behave
with function parameters passed by value, we established the following rule:

> **Rule:** In a temporary materialization, if the `reloc` operand is a function parameter passed by value, and its
type provides an eligible move constructor, then the relocation constructor
(if any) may be discarded during overload resolution. Whether the relocation
constructor is discarded is implementation-defined.

If the ABI is not already compliant, then this rule will force an ABI change
on functions which have parameters passed by value,
and whose type declares a relocation constructor but no move constructor.

The good news is as of today, there are no such types, so no existing function
should suffer from an ABI break. However, some types may accidently gain a relocation
constructor and may trigger an ABI break nonetheless.

In a future library changes proposal to support relocation (separate from this one),
we must be careful not to
make any existing type relocate-only, especially for that concern.
However we will add a relocation constructor on many classes, alongside their
existing copy and move constructors. In doing so,
some of them may fit the criteria for ABI break, should their copy and move constructors
be deleted.

One example is a function with signature: `void foo(gsl::non_null<std::unique_ptr<int>>);`,
supposing `unique_ptr` gets a relocation constructor.
In turn GSL developers will
likely add a relocation constructor too. That makes `gsl::non_null<std::unique_ptr<int>>`
relocate-only, while it wasn't before, and may cause a potential ABI break.

There is zero value of passing a `gsl::non_null<std::unique_ptr>` by value
to a function today, so we doubt anyone would write such a function.
However those functions might theoritically exist, and might have an ABI change.

Also, library vendors are encouraged to migrate to an ABI where any function that
takes non-trivial parameters by value are responsible for their destruction.
Then, the function definitions can make the most out of `reloc`.
This is not required by the proposal.

We believe it's up to the implementation to choose what they want to do with their ABI:
- *full break*, use callee-destroy or equivalent for all non-trivial relocatable types
    passed by value (for those who don't care about ABI) ;
- *break with opt-out*: a relocation constructor attribute to opt-out of the ABI
    break on functions where it is passed be value.
    This solution should also provide propagation mechanisms suitable for composition
    (these could be standardized at a later date) ;
- *likely no break, but opt-in*: an improved `[[trivial_abi]]` that actually checks that
    the type is trivially relocatable ;
- *likely no break*: use callee-destroy only for relocatable but immovable types ;
- and those that are callee-destroy already don't need to do anything!

In all cases the following mitigation and migration techniques could be employed:

- functions that have an ABI change could be mangled differently. This makes
    ABI breakage detectable ;
- for such functions, up to two symbols are emitted, where the old
    symbol is emitted only if the function does not in actual fact relocate from
    its parameters, in which case the new symbol is emitted, and its implementation
    forwards to the old and then destructs its relocatable parameters on exit ;

Last, this potential breakage was discussed on
[Itanium ABI github](https://github.com/itanium-cxx-abi/cxx-abi/issues/187),
although we didn't get much feedback.

### prvalue assignment operator ### {#prvalue-assign-op-abi}

As mentioned above, if the class-type is relocatable but immovable, then it may have an
impact on existing prvalue-assignment operators (like it does to any function).
However this change is purely opt-in. If there is an existing prvalue-assignment
operator in a class, then it will prevent the implicit declaration of the
relocation constructor, which will in turn prevent from the potential ABI break.

Also, the relocation assignment operator may be aliased.
If aliasing occurs, then the ABI does not break as aliasing happens only on a
new hidden function.

### The virtual slicing function ### {#virtual-slicing-func-discussion}

The introduction of a new virtual function is an ABI break. For this reason,
the new virtual slicing function is entirely opt-in. It can only be triggered
by an explicit declaration of a relocation constructor. This seems suitable,
as a declaring a relocation constructor may already have an impact on existing
ABIs.

We identified two areas of concern about virtual slicing functions:

1. the function is hidden from the developers ;
1. developers may lose a bit of control over the vtable, as the slicing function
    is forcibly declared as a side effect of other declarations ;

Both concerns could be addressed by declaring the virtual slicing function
differently.

We could allow the relocation constructor to be declared as `virtual`,
and doing so would generate the slicing function. Hence the declaration of the
slicing function would no longer be hidden, and changes on the vtable are
explicit.

However this solution creates other issues:

- Although the relocation constructor sort of acts as a destructor, declaring a
    constructor as virtual is unusual in C++. Besides, overridden
    relocation constructors in derived classes will have a non-matching first
    parameter compared to the base class. (If the base class declares
    `virtual B(B reloc)`, then the derived class declares `D(D reloc) override`.)
- If the slicing function is not declared, it may cause unsafe object slicing
    situations and resource leaks. The situation where we want to support this
    kind of safe slicing is when dealing with a polymorphic object that can be
    destructed from a base class pointer. If the language declares the virtual
    slicing function automatically, then developers cannot get it wrong.


A third solution would be to declare the virtual slicing function as any other
function:

```c++
struct T
{
    virtual auto operator slice() = default;
};
```

But this creates other issues:

- The name of the function needs not to clash with any existing function ;
- The return value and parameters are *implementation-defined*, so hardly writable
    by the developers ;
- This is an explicit function declaration for which no definition must be
    supplied ;
- And the safety point stands: developers may forget to declare it and unsafe
    object slicing may occur silently.

For all those reasons, and because we believe that safety in the language should
come first, we proposed the virtual slicing function to be hidden and
automatically declared if the criteria are met.

## Impact of relocation on input parameter passing ## {#parameter-passing}

C++ provides multple ways to pass parameters to functions, and we expect
relocation to have a small impact on the existing practices.
We will focus only on the input parameter passing, as output parameters are
unaffected by relocation.

There are three ways to pass input parameters to a function:

- by const reference `T const&`: the function will not modify this parameter.
    This is mainly used when the object is not cheap to copy, and hence better
    pased by constant reference.
    Ownership transfer is not possible with constant references.
- by rvalue reference `T&&`: this is used as an input parameter, while
    paradoxically the function will likely modify it (leaving it in a
    moved-from state). This form of parameter passing explicitly indicates
    an ownership transfer, although the transfer may not happen
    (see `std::map::try_emplace`).
- by value `T`: this parameter passing is favored against the other two
    if the cost of the object creation (copy-or-move-constructed) is cheap.
    This form of parameter passing is less seen in APIs that deal with a
    generic template type (see `std::vector::push_back`).
    This form can be used for ownership transfer, and is the only form that
    allows relocation.

The "by value" parameter passing will gain more attention as it is the only
proposed way to perform relocation. We advise:

- to pass by value instead of by rvalue reference if the class-type provides a
    relocation constructor ;
- to keep passing by rvalue reference if the class-type provides a
    move constructor and no relocation constructor ;
- generic APIs should preferably provide the three overloads (by value,
    by rvalue reference, by const reference). Alternatively, only the "by const
    reference" and the "by value" overloads could be enough, as the "by value"
    form allows ownership transfer either with move or relocation.

### Compared performance of reloc vs std::move for ownership transfer ### {#reloc-vs-move-perf-comparison}

This section will compare the performance differences between `std::move` and
`reloc`, across the different input parameter passing policies.
As `std::move` cannot be used with parameters passed by constant references,
this form will be left out.

Let's consider a common scenario. We have an automatic variable `src`, and we
want to transfer its resources to some other system. This is achieved by a series
of function calls, where each function will forward the source object to the next,
until it reaches the target system. The object is always forwarded either
by `std::move` across the whole chain, either by `reloc`. Each function may
take its input parameter either by value, or by rvalue reference.
Imagine that each function is defined in different translation units.
We will measure the performance in terms of object creation.

<table>
<tr><th>std::move (by rvalue ref)</th><th>relocation (by value)</th></tr>
<tr>
<td>
```c++
void foo()
{
    T src = getT();
    sink_1(std::move(src));
}
void sink_1(T&& src)
{
    sink_2(std::move(src));
}
void sink_2(T&& src)
{
    sink_3(std::move(src));
}
// ...
void sink_N(T&& src);
```
A single object is created, in `foo`.
</td>
<td>
```c++
void foo()
{
    T const src = getT();
    sink_1(reloc src);
}
void sink_1(T src)
{
    sink_2(reloc src);
}
void sink_2(T src)
{
    sink_3(reloc src);
}
// ...
void sink_N(T src);
```
Depending on the [implementation](#0-copy) (if relocation elision can happen
all the way), we have a single object creation
in `foo`,
or *N* object creations (one in each function body).
</td>
</tr>

<tr><th>std::move (by value)</th><th>relocation (by rvalue ref)</th></tr>
<tr>
<td>
```c++
void foo()
{
    T src = getT();
    sink_1(std::move(src));
}
void sink_1(T src)
{
    sink_2(std::move(src));
}
void sink_2(T src)
{
    sink_3(std::move(src));
}
// ...
void sink_N(T src);
```
*N* objects are created, one in each function body.
</td>
<td>
```c++
void foo()
{
    T const src = getT();
    sink_1(reloc src);
}
void sink_1(T&& src)
{
    sink_2(reloc src);
}
void sink_2(T&& src)
{
    sink_3(reloc src);
}
// ...
void sink_N(T&& src);
```
This design may seem a bit peculiar, but may happen during an aggressive search
and replace in a code base to replace `std::move` calls to `reloc`.

In `foo`'s body, the `reloc src` expression promotes `src` to a prvalue.
As `src` is *early destructible*, the temporary materialization is elided
and the prvalue is directly converted to an xvalue. After the full expression
evaluation, `foo` will implicitly invoke `src`'s destructor.
Successive `reloc` expressions in the
`sink_i` functions will operate from an xvalue `src`, performing xvalue
forwarding as if by `std::move`.
We have a single object creation.
</td>
</tr>

</table>

You can see that in the worst case, `reloc` is not worse than `std::move` if all
functions are "by value".
It has the same performance as `std::move` if all
functions are "by rvalue reference" (but then no relocation can be done),
or if all functions are "by value" and the implementation can elide all the
relocations. This is why we say that `reloc` and `std::move` provide overall
a similar performance, one performing better than the other in specific situations.

### Achieving 0-copy transfer with a cooperative ABI ### {#0-copy}

Let `f` be a function which takes an object of non-trivial type `T` as value parameter:
`void f(T);`. To call `f`, the Itanium ABI
chooses to pass the parameter by address. The following happens in the caller:

1. a temporary object is created, initialized from the expression passed in the
    matching parameter slot of the function call expression.
1. `f` is called, passing the address of the temporary object instead
    of the whole object bytefield.
1. when the function returns (normally or through an exception), the temporary
    object is destroyed.

Now, let us say that `T` has migrated to relocation semantics: it has a
relocation constructor, but no move constructor. This alone makes any object of
type `T` *early destructible*, and eligible to relocation elision.
Hence, with the Itanium ABI and relocation elision,
the expression `f(reloc obj)` translates
into a call to `f`, merely forwarding the address of `obj`. No temporaries are
created.

For instance, in the following snippet:
```c++
void vector<T>::push_back(T x) // putative std::vector push_back
{
  ensures_space();
  new (_end++) T{reloc x};
}

void bar(T x)
{
  get_vector().push_back(reloc x);
}

void foo(T x)
{
  if (test()) bar(reloc x);
}

// in some function:
T x;
foo(reloc x);
```

Translates into this pseudo-C++ code:
```c++
void vector<T>::push_back(T* x)
{
  ensures_space();
  memcpy(_end++, x, sizeof(T)); // given that relocation is trivial
}

void bar(T* x)
{
  get_vector().push_back(x);
}

void foo(T* x)
{
  if (test()) bar(x);
  else x->~T();
}

// in some function:
T x;
foo(&x);
// x destructor is not called
```

This allows the object to be created once, and forwarded by address until it
is relocated inside a vector. No useless copies are created.
Yet no new value categories were introduced, all happened with value-semantics,
and the code is safe as static analysis ensures the object is not reused after
`reloc`. This can be achieved only because of relocation elision and the fact
that value parameters are passed by address in Itanium ABI.

This proposal does not mandate this implementation, we only highlight that such
an implementation is possible. Also, if this proposal is approved, it will create
an opportunity for other ABIs to migrate to something similar,
should they wish to benefit from this optimization.

### About clang's trivial_abi ### {#clang-trivial-abi}

Consider the following `void foo(T obj);`. `foo` takes `obj` by value.
In Itanium ABI, it translate differently in terms of ABI
depending whether `T` is trivial or not:

- If trivial, then `obj` is passed by value as expected, meaning that a copy of
    `obj` is constructed in `foo`'s parameter slot memory space.
    clang’s trivial_abi states that `foo` body then owns `obj` which makes
    `obj` *early destructible*.
- Otherwise, as [pointed out above](#0-copy), a copy of `obj` is constructed
    as a temporary object, and the address of that temporary is passed to `foo`.
    Then `obj` in `foo` body is not *early destructible*.

clang's `trivial_abi` is a non-standard class attribute. It forces the
class-type to be considered as trivial for value parameter passing.

Is this attribute helpful for relocation? Yes and no.
On one hand, this makes the object *early destructible* which enables
relocation. But on the other hand, the object is passed down to `foo` (not its
address), which prevents relocation elision of `obj` in `foo` body.

The cooperative ABI discussed above is arguably better (pass by address,
and give ownership). This enables both relocation and its elision, ... but at the
cost of having to dereference the `obj` pointer when the object is accessed.

Note that this paper does not enforce one parameter passing or the other, we
only mandate `obj` to be *early destructible* if `T` has a relocation constructor
and no move constructor (so the second call convention is amended for those
types).

## std::exchange and std::swap ## {#std-exchange-swap}

We have long hesitated to propose new overloads to `std::exchange` and `std::swap`,
which would use relocation semantics instead of move, further improving
support for relocate-only types.

Such an `std::exchange` overload would be:
```c++
template <class T>
requires std::is_nothrow_relocation_constructible_v<T> &&
std::is_final_v<T>
constexpr T exchange(T& obj, T new_value) noexcept
{
    T const old{std::reloc_and_uninitialize(std::addressof(obj))};
    std::construct_at(std::addressof(obj), reloc new_value);
    return old;
}
```

This function works by destroying `obj` and relocating `new_value` on top of it.
You will have noticed the bunch of requirements:

- The relocation constructor must not throw. Should an exception be emitted
    by a relocation constructor, then `exchange` would leave `obj` in an
    uninitialized state, opening the door for future disasters.
- To prevent from reconstructing over `obj` with a different type, `T` must be final.
    For instance this is prohibited:
    ```c++
    class D : public B { std::string _str; };

    D obj;
    B old = std::exchange(static_cast<B&>(obj), B{});
    // obj destructor will attempt to destroy `_str` which is uninitialized memory.
    ```

The same set of requirements would apply to `std::swap`. We feel that the requirements
are too strong to ever be practical. In particular, no STL classes use the `final`
specifier, and frameworks in general are as unlikely to use it,
which greatly diminishes the usefulness of such overloads.

Relocation-based and byteswap-based versions of `std::exchange` and `std::swap` may
be implemented and used by library vendors, to grant a performance boost and
improved support for relocate-only types. Those overloads can be used safely
in controled environments (for instance in container implementations, where there
is no surprise in the dynamic type of the stored object).

### Relocating data members ### {#reloc-data-members}

`std::exchange` and `std::swap` with relocation semantics are not proposed,
to the detriment of relocate-only type support. This is why
we recommend every relocate-only type to provide an `exchange` member function,
as an alternative to `std::exchange` but with relocation semantics.
Such a function enables to relocate a data member (or any non-complete object)
similarly to what is done today with `std::move`.

```c++
struct A
{
    std::box<T> take_box() { return box_.exchange({}); }
    // [...]
};

std::pair<int, std::box<T>> p = getPair();
foo(p.second.exchange({}));
```

Such an `exchange` function can be implemented **without resorting to destroy
and reconstruct**, as it can take advantage of the private access to
lift off some blocking class invariants.

NOTE: the `exchange` member function is to be used when the data member is to be reused.
If it belongs to an object that is about to be destroyed, then object decomposition
is the preferred approach as the cost to reconstruct a valid state is avoided.

```c++
struct A
{
    std::box<T> take_box(this A self reloc) { return self.box_; }
    // [...]
};

std::pair<int, std::box<T>> p reloc = getPair();
foo(reloc p.second);
```

## Object decomposition alternatives ## {#object-decomposition-alt}

In R3, object decomposition took the form of "decomposing functions". An object
could only be decomposed through a member function:

```c++
class T {
    // [..]
    auto extract(this T reloc) { return _obj; }
private:
    reloc_only_t _obj;
};
```

This had drawbacks:

- As objects could only be decomposed through member functions, it implied that:
    - Decomposition had to happen through a dedicated function ;
    - Decomposition had to happen within the class-type ;
- It was hard to decompose more than one object at a given time ;
- This used a weird syntax to access base classes: `reloc B`, with `B` being
    a base class.

We wanted decomposition to be more convenient to use, as it is an intrinsically
close concept to relocation. It took several attempts to reach what we propose
in this paper.

### The dot-reloc operator ### {#dot-reloc-take-1}

We studied for a while a putative *dot-reloc* operator: `p.reloc first`.
The first time the expression was evaluated, the object used as the left operand
would become decomposed, and the subobject used as the right operand would be relocated.
Once decomposed, the source object could no longer be used normally. We
restricted the expressions where a decomposed object would be allowed, down to:

- data member access ;
- cast to base class ;
- base member function call ;

For instance:

```c++
pair<string, string> p = getPair();
if (test()) {
    foo(p.reloc first); // p is now decomposed, p.first is relocated
    bar(p.second); // ok
}
foobar(p); // ko: p may be decomposed
```
And it could even work with pointer to data-members, given that they were
constant-evaluated:
```c++
template <auto DataMember>
string Get(pair<string, string> p)
{
    return p.*reloc DataMember;
}
```

This approach proved quite convenient to use. However it suffured from much added
complexity:
1. the code could be hard to follow because objects would be decomposed only when
    the first `.reloc` operator was evaluated ;
1. the static information of the object (for instance, whether some expressions
    involving the object are valid), which are supposed to remain
    unchanged during the program ... would change when the first `.reloc`
    operator was evaluated ;
1. relocating a base class still has the unusual syntax: `src.reloc BaseClass` ;
1. lots of new rules to learn on how to handle a decomposed object ;
1. would complicate code parsing, as the validity of an id-expression designing an
    object would depend on the surrounding expressions, should this object
    be decomposed or potentially decomposed.

This motivated us to make a take a dractically different approach.

### The namespace approach ### {#namespace-reloc-take-2}

Most of the issues raised above come from the fact that a decomposed object
could still be used as an object. This made code parsing complicated, especially
considering that an object could become decomposed from within a code branch,
so it could not be known at compile-time whether the object was decomposed.

For this reason, we made two changes:

- an object cannot be decomposed on the fly,
    it needs to be declared as decomposed, and ;
- a decomposed object is no longer an object, and can only be used as a proxy
    to designate its direct data member and base classes.

The main idea was that the decomposed object would be exploded into a dedicated
special namespace, named after the object name. For instance:

```c++
pair<string, string> p reloc = getPair();
// p becomes a namespace containing all its direct subobjects
if (test()) {
    foo(reloc p::first);
    bar(p::second);
}
foobar(p); // ko: p is not a value

struct D : public B {};
D d reloc = D{};
foo(reloc d::B); // relocates base class
```

This approach addressed all the points raised above, while still being
convenient to use. There were two aspects where it compared unfavorably to
*dot-reloc*,
is that for one it could not relocate a subobject from a pointer to data member, or from a
base class whose name is passed as a template type parameter:

```c++
template <string T::*_str>
string GetStr(T obj reloc)
{
    return obj::*_str; // invalid expression
}
template <class B>
B GetBase(T obj reloc)
{
    return obj::B; // invalid expression
}
```

Second, the namespace syntax was too unusual for C++, so a third approach, taking
the best of the two was picked.

### The proposed approach ### {#dot-reloc-no-longer}

We believe the proposed approach addresses all the points raised above, while still being
convenient to use. This approach is close to the namespace approach, except that
a dot operator is used instead of the namespace resolution operator (which feels
nore natural to access data members). And this syntax makes it easier to
support relocation of a data member through a constant-evaluated pointer-to-member.

Code parsing is not made more complex, as the object must be declared as decomposed,
and the decomposed object does not yield a value. There is still the unsual
`obj.Base` syntax, (which is made possible as `obj` is decomposed and is not a value)
but we consider this to be a minor issue.

## Why a new keyword? ## {#reloc-new-keyword}

Alternatively, a new series of symbols could be used instead of introducing
a new keyword, like: `<~< obj` or `&< obj` in place of `reloc obj`.
However, we feel like `reloc obj` better conveys the intent, and has better
readability.

The introduction of a new keyword may always break existing codebases. We had a
look at several well-known open source C++ projects to analyse what volume of code
would break if `reloc` were a keyword.

For each of the following repositories, we searched for the `reloc` string,
at word boundaries, with case-sensitivity, in all C++ source files and headers
(`*.cc`, `*.cpp`, `*.cxx`, `*.h`, `*.hpp`, `*.hh`).
We manually discarded matches that
were not code (comments or strings). And we put that in perspective with the
total number of files, lines and words of the repository.

- [Qt](https://github.com/qt/qtbase): 0 hits; files:  7,586 ; lines:  2,794,607 ;
    words:  98,635,622; commit: 040b4a4b21b3
- [boost](https://github.com/boostorg/boost) (with all submodules): 0 hits;
    files:  23,726 ; lines:  4,133,844 ;
